B.XU=s(["Anterior longitudinal ligament and anterior half of vertebral body","Posterior half of vertebral body and posterior longitudinal ligament","Pedicles, facets, lamina, and spinous processes","Nucleus pulposus and annulus fibrosus"],t.s)
B.a9M=new A.e("According to the Denis 3-column model, what defines the middle column of the spine?",B.XU,1,"The middle column consists of the posterior half of the vertebral body and the posterior longitudinal ligament (PLL). It is the linchpin for stability - if it fails, the spine is unstable.")
B.pJ=s(["10%","25%","50%","75%"],t.s)
B.a9Q=new A.e("What percentage of anterior vertebral height loss is the threshold for conservative vs. potentially surgical management of compression fractures?",B.pJ,1,"Less than 25% anterior height loss with intact middle and posterior columns is generally treated conservatively. Greater height loss may require surgical intervention.")
B.YC=s(["Anterior column failure only","Failure of both the anterior and middle columns with possible retropulsion of fragments into the spinal canal","Posterior column failure only","Disc herniation without bony injury"],t.s)
B.acd=new A.e("What key finding differentiates a burst fracture from a simple compression fracture?",B.YC,1,"A burst fracture involves failure of both the anterior and middle columns (vs. only anterior in compression fractures), with retropulsion of bone fragments into the spinal canal threatening the cord.")
B.XP=s(["MRI showing facet hypertrophy","CT myelogram","Double diagnostic medial branch block with concordant relief","Bone scan"],t.s)
B.a87=new A.e("What is the gold standard for diagnosing facet (zygapophyseal) joint pain?",B.XP,2,"The double diagnostic block is the gold standard. Two separate blocks with different anesthetics are performed; if the patient gets >80% relief both times with duration matching the drug used, the diagnosis is confirmed. MRI findings correlate poorly with pain.")
B.Xc=s(["Warmth, redness, and swelling","A palpable taut band, referred pain pattern, and local twitch response (jump sign)","Crepitus, locking, and catching","Numbness, tingling, and weakness"],t.s)
B.a4S=new A.e("What are the three pathognomonic features of a myofascial trigger point?",B.Xc,1,"A trigger point has: (1) a palpable taut band, (2) referred pain to a distant area when pressed, and (3) a local twitch response (jump sign) when the band is snapped. This distinguishes it from a simple tender point.")
