B.a49=new A.e("Morton's neuroma most commonly affects which intermetatarsal space?",B.Pm,2,"Morton's neuroma most commonly affects the third-fourth intermetatarsal space (between the 3rd and 4th metatarsal heads). It involves the interdigital nerve and presents with burning pain, numbness in adjacent toes, and a positive Mulder's click test.")
B.V1=s(["Pain at the Achilles insertion that worsens with activity","Heel pain that is worst with the first steps in the morning","Forefoot pain that worsens with toe walking","Lateral ankle pain with weight bearing"],t.s)
B.a6r=new A.e("Plantar fasciitis pain is classically described as:",B.V1,1,"Plantar fasciitis presents with medial heel pain at the plantar fascial origin on the calcaneal tubercle. It is classically worst with the first steps in the morning (post-static dyskinesia) and after prolonged sitting, then gradually improves with walking.")
B.PO=s(["Jones fracture","March fracture","Lisfranc fracture","Dancer's fracture"],t.s)
B.ac8=new A.e("A stress fracture of the second metatarsal is also known as a:",B.PO,1,'A "march fracture" is a stress fracture of the second (or third) metatarsal shaft, classically seen in military recruits during basic training. A Jones fracture is at the base of the 5th metatarsal. A dancer\'s fracture is a spiral fracture of the 5th metatarsal shaft.')
B.VI=s(["Talus and calcaneus","Metatarsal bases and tarsal bones (cuneiforms and cuboid)","Phalanges and metatarsal heads","Navicular and cuneiforms"],t.s)
B.abj=new A.e("The Lisfranc joint complex involves the articulation between which structures?",B.VI,1,"The Lisfranc joint is the tarsometatarsal joint complex where the metatarsal bases articulate with the cuneiform bones and cuboid. Lisfranc injuries are commonly missed on initial X-rays. The key ligament runs from the medial cuneiform to the base of the second metatarsal.")
B.QH=s(["Lateral deviation of the great toe","Degenerative arthritis of the first MTP joint with limited dorsiflexion","Inflammation of the sesamoid bones","A fracture of the hallux proximal phalanx"],t.s)
B.a6b=new A.e("Hallux rigidus refers to:",B.QH,1,"Hallux rigidus is osteoarthritis of the first MTP joint characterized by pain and limited dorsiflexion (normally 65-75 degrees). It is the most common arthritic condition of the foot. Hallux valgus (bunion) is lateral deviation of the great toe \u2014 a different condition.")
B.Og=s([B.a49,B.a6r,B.ac8,B.abj,B.a6b],t.b)
B.IY=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Foot",B.Og)
B.ZJ=s(["Periarticular osteopenia","Pencil-in-cup deformity","Symmetric joint space narrowing","Marginal erosions with pannus"],t.s)
B.a7E=new A.e("What is the classic radiographic finding of psoriatic arthritis that distinguishes it from RA?",B.ZJ,1,"The pencil-in-cup deformity is a signature radiographic finding of psoriatic arthritis, where the distal phalanx erodes to a point (pencil) sitting in the widened base of the adjacent phalanx (cup).")
B.NN=s(["They cause liver failure in HIV patients","They severely tank CD4 counts and can trigger opportunistic infections or Kaposi sarcoma","They interact with antiretroviral medications","They cause severe skin reactions in HIV"],t.s)
B.a4_=new A.e("Why are methotrexate and oral corticosteroids contraindicated in HIV-positive patients with psoriatic arthritis?",B.NN,1,"Methotrexate is a potent immunosuppressant that can severely reduce CD4 counts, leading to opportunistic infections. Oral steroids can also tank immunity and trigger Kaposi sarcoma flares in HIV patients.")
B.XE=s(["Low white blood cell count","Normal glucose level","Crystals present","Bloody fluid"],t.s)
B.a4s=new A.e("In reactive arthritis, what synovial fluid finding helps rule out septic arthritis?",B.XE,1,"In reactive arthritis, the joint itself is sterile (it is an immune reaction to a distant infection), so synovial fluid glucose remains normal. In septic arthritis, bacteria consume glucose, causing it to plummet.")
B.X6=s(["Myositis","Malar rash that spares the nasolabial folds","Mucosal dryness","Meningitis"],t.s)
B.a8E=new A.e("What is the DOPAMINE RASH mnemonic criterion 'M' for in SLE diagnosis?",B.X6,1,"M stands for Malar rash, the classic butterfly rash across the cheeks and bridge of the nose. The critical detail is that it spares the nasolabial folds; if redness extends into those creases, consider rosacea instead.")
