B.acl=new A.e("What is the hallmark physical exam finding that confirms myofascial pain syndrome?",B.NU,2,"The twitch response is the hallmark \u2014 a brisk, involuntary focal contraction of muscle fibers within the taut band, elicited by snapping across the muscle.")
B.X8=s(["Too expensive","Not more effective than other injections and can be myotoxic","They cause systemic immunosuppression","They worsen the taut band"],t.s)
B.a85=new A.e("Why are corticosteroid injections contraindicated for myofascial trigger points?",B.X8,1,"Corticosteroid injections have not been shown to be more effective for myofascial pain and can be myotoxic, causing additional muscle damage.")
B.Ur=s(["Sumatriptan","Indomethacin","Propranolol","Verapamil"],t.s)
B.a6G=new A.e("What medication response differentiates hemicrania continua from cluster headaches?",B.Ur,1,"Hemicrania continua and paroxysmal hemicrania have an absolute and complete response to indomethacin, while cluster headaches do not.")
B.MA=s(["Oral sumatriptan","Indomethacin","High-flow 100% oxygen via non-rebreather mask","IV corticosteroids"],t.s)
B.a62=new A.e("What is the first-line abortive treatment for acute cluster headaches?",B.MA,2,"100% oxygen at 7-12 liters per minute via non-rebreather mask for about 15 minutes is the acute abortive treatment of choice.")
B.T8=s(["Two of four","Three of four","All four","One of four"],t.s)
B.adc=new A.e("Per the Budapest criteria, how many symptom categories must a patient report symptoms in to meet CRPS diagnostic criteria?",B.T8,1,"Budapest criteria require at least one symptom in three of four categories: sensory, vasomotor, sudomotor/edema, and motor/trophic.")
B.OR=s([B.acl,B.a85,B.a6G,B.a62,B.adc],t.b)
B.J3=new A.a8("Pain 2",B.OR)
B.Pr=s(["Pneumothorax","Spinal cord infarction or stroke from arterial embolization","Epidural hematoma","Dural puncture headache"],t.s)
B.a4N=new A.e("What is the most dangerous major complication of cervical spine injections involving particulate steroid injection into an artery?",B.Pr,1,"If particulate steroid crystals are injected into a vertebral artery, they act as emboli that clog small vessels, causing spinal cord infarction, stroke, or blindness. This is why the margin for error is non-existent in cervical procedures.")
B.R8=s(["It is constant regardless of position","It improves when standing","It is positional - severe when standing, relieved when lying flat","It only occurs at night"],t.s)
B.adi=new A.e("What is the hallmark distinguishing feature of a dural puncture (post-dural puncture) headache?",B.R8,2,"A dural puncture headache is positional: severe when standing (gravity pulls the brain down due to lost CSF cushion, stretching meninges) and relieved when lying flat. This positional nature is the diagnostic key.")
B.V3=s(["Infections increase pain sensitivity","Bacteria can be introduced into the epidural space causing an epidural abscess","Infections cause blood thinning","Steroids are less effective during infection"],t.s)
B.aaq=new A.e("Why is active infection anywhere in the body an absolute contraindication to spinal injection procedures?",B.V3,1,"The epidural space is highly vascular and a potential space for bacteria to grow. Introducing bacteria can cause an epidural abscess that compresses the spinal cord, leading to paralysis - a surgical emergency.")
B.V7=s(["Diabetes causes spinal stenosis","Corticosteroids raise blood sugar and can push poorly controlled diabetics into hyperglycemic crisis, plus high sugar levels increase infection risk","Diabetic patients are allergic to contrast dye","Diabetes causes osteoporosis of the spine"],t.s)
B.a9b=new A.e("Why must uncontrolled diabetes be screened for before performing spinal steroid injections?",B.V7,1,"Steroids induce gluconeogenesis, raising blood sugar. In uncontrolled diabetics, this can trigger hyperglycemic crisis. Additionally, high sugar levels paralyze neutrophils, increasing infection risk from the procedure.")
B.a_y=s(["Continue the procedure quickly to finish","Administer epinephrine","Stop the procedure, remove the needle, place patient in Trendelenburg position (feet up, head down)","Give oral fluids and wait"],t.s)
B.a9l=new A.e("What is the immediate management protocol if a patient develops a vasovagal episode during a spinal procedure?",B.a_y,2,"Stop the procedure immediately, remove the needle, and place the patient in Trendelenburg position (supine with feet higher than head by 15-30 degrees) to use gravity to return blood to the brain. Increase IV fluids.")
B.OT=s([B.a4N,B.adi,B.aaq,B.a9b,B.a9l],t.b)
B.J4=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Spine 6",B.OT)
B.RB=s(["Hip width plus 2 inches","Hip width exactly (no extra space)","Hip width plus 1 inch","Shoulder width"],t.s)
B.aa7=new A.e("For a manual wheelchair, the seat width should equal:",B.RB,1,"For manual wheelchairs, seat width equals hip width with no extra space. This keeps the wheels as close to the body as possible for efficient propulsion and reduced shoulder strain.")
B.Pa=s(["The seat bounces during propulsion","Gravity pulls the hips into internal rotation and adduction, forcing posterior pelvic tilt and sacral sitting","The seat stretches over time becoming too wide","The patient slides forward due to low friction"],t.s)
B.ab9=new A.e("What is the hammocking effect caused by a vinyl sling seat?",B.Pa,1,"A sling seat sags like a hammock, pulling the hips into internal rotation/adduction, causing posterior pelvic tilt, sacral sitting, kyphosis, and increased pressure injury risk.")
B.Oz=s(["Zero (flush contact)","Less than 3 inches","Exactly 5 inches","1 inch or less"],t.s)
B.a8c=new A.e("The gap between the front of the wheelchair seat and the back of the knee should be:",B.Oz,1,"The gap should be less than 3 inches. Too shallow concentrates weight on the ischial tuberosities (pressure injury risk). Too deep compresses the popliteal area causing the patient to slide forward.")
B.SD=s(["Add 5 inches for clearance","Do NOT add the 3-4 inches of ground clearance used in standard chairs","Double the cushion height","Use standing height instead of leg length"],t.s)
B.a7k=new A.e("For a hemi-height (foot-propelled) wheelchair, how does the seat height formula differ from standard?",B.SD,1,"For foot propellers, the formula is leg length minus compressed cushion height only. Adding ground clearance would leave the feet dangling, unable to reach the floor.")
B.UV=s(["Hip width minus 1 inch","Hip width exactly","Hip width plus approximately 1 inch","Shoulder width plus 2 inches"],t.s)
B.ac7=new A.e("For a power wheelchair, the seat width rule differs from manual. What is it?",B.UV,2,"Power chair users are not propelling with their arms, so comfort is prioritized. The rule is hip width plus approximately 1 inch for breathing room and weight shifting.")
B.Z7=s([B.aa7,B.ab9,B.a8c,B.a7k,B.ac7],t.b)
B.J5=new A.a8("Wheelchair 1",B.Z7)
B.XF=s(["Adolescents","Children under 5 years","Adults over 40","Elderly patients"],t.s)
B.a53=new A.e("Nursemaid's elbow (radial head subluxation) most commonly occurs in which age group?",B.XF,1,"Nursemaid's elbow occurs most commonly in children under 5 years old due to their relatively undeveloped annular ligament. It is caused by a sudden pull on the extended, pronated arm. Reduction involves supination with elbow flexion.")
B.WM=s(["Wrist drop with loss of sensation","Finger drop (inability to extend fingers at MCP joints) without sensory loss","Numbness in the thumb and index finger","Weakness of elbow flexion"],t.s)
B.a3T=new A.e("Posterior interosseous nerve (PIN) syndrome at the elbow presents with:",B.WM,1,"PIN syndrome causes weakness of finger and thumb extension at the MCP joints without sensory loss (PIN is a motor branch). Wrist extension is partially preserved (ECRL is innervated before the PIN). The Arcade of Frohse is the most common compression site.")
B.Xo=s(["Anterior elbow crease","Lateral epicondyle","Posterior tip of the elbow","Medial epicondyle"],t.s)
B.a8f=new A.e("Olecranon bursitis is characterized by swelling at which location?",B.Xo,2,'The olecranon bursa lies superficially over the posterior tip of the olecranon process. Bursitis causes visible swelling at this site ("student\'s elbow" or "miner\'s elbow"). It can be traumatic, infectious (septic), or due to gout/RA. Always aspirate if infection is suspected.')
B.SG=s(["Lateral epicondyle fracture, UCL tear, radial head fracture","Elbow dislocation, radial head fracture, coronoid process fracture","Olecranon fracture, UCL tear, radial nerve injury","Supracondylar fracture, radial head fracture, ulnar nerve injury"],t.s)
B.abC=new A.e('The "terrible triad" of the elbow includes which three injuries?',B.SG,1,'The "terrible triad" = posterior elbow dislocation + radial head fracture + coronoid process fracture. It has a high rate of instability and poor outcomes. Surgical fixation of both fractures plus LCL repair is typically required.')
B.XJ=s(["Lateral epicondylitis","Olecranon bursitis","Elbow dislocation or fracture-dislocation","Cubital tunnel syndrome"],t.s)
B.a79=new A.e("Heterotopic ossification (HO) at the elbow is most commonly seen after which type of injury?",B.XJ,2,"HO at the elbow is most common after elbow dislocations and fracture-dislocations, especially with associated head injuries or burns. Prophylaxis with indomethacin or low-dose radiation may be considered in high-risk patients. Surgical excision is delayed until the bone matures.")
B.U9=s([B.a53,B.a3T,B.a8f,B.abC,B.a79],t.b)
B.J6=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Elbow 2",B.U9)
B.SH=s(["Warm, soft, textured","Cool, rigid, smooth","Elastic and compressive","Rough and heated"],t.s)
B.a6v=new A.e("In orthokinetics, what type of material creates a passive (inhibitory) field to calm spastic muscles?",B.SH,1,"A passive field uses cool, rigid, and smooth materials for an inhibitory effect on spastic muscles.")
B.YL=s(["Full extension","30-45 degrees flexion","70-90 degrees flexion","90-110 degrees flexion"],t.s)
B.a9_=new A.e("What is the correct MCP joint position in a resting hand splint (intrinsic plus)?",B.YL,2,"MCPs must be flexed 70-90 degrees to keep collateral ligaments stretched to maximum length.")
B.a_A=s(["Finger flexion","Finger extension","Wrist extension","Intrinsic hand muscles"],t.s)
B.a60=new A.e("A C6 tetraplegic can use a wrist-driven prehension orthosis because they retain what key function?",B.a_A,2,"C6 retains wrist extension (ECRL). The splint harnesses tenodesis: active wrist extension drives fingers into a three-jaw chuck pinch.")
B.Ss=s(["1/5 (trace)","2/5 (poor)","3/5 (fair)","4/5 (good)"],t.s)
B.acr=new A.e("What minimum muscle strength is required in the biceps for a balanced forearm orthosis (BFO)?",B.Ss,1,"The BFO requires MMT of 2/5 in biceps and pectoralis \u2014 it removes gravity so minimal strength becomes functional.")
B.Qg=s(["Too heavy","The constant bouncy stretch triggers the stretch reflex and worsens spasticity","Not enough force to overcome tone","Causes skin breakdown"],t.s)
B.a57=new A.e("Why should dynamic spring-loaded extension splints NOT be used on a spastic elbow?",B.Qg,1,"Dynamic spring splints trigger muscle spindles and the stretch reflex, increasing spastic tone. Static solid pressure is preferred.")
B.VW=s([B.a6v,B.a9_,B.a60,B.acr,B.a57],t.b)
B.J7=new A.a8("Prosthetics & Orthotics 9",B.VW)
B.Zv=s(["Adolescent athletes",'Middle-aged "weekend warriors"',"Elderly sedentary patients","Professional athletes"],t.s)
B.ad6=new A.e("Achilles tendon rupture classically occurs in which demographic?",B.Zv,1,'Achilles ruptures classically occur in middle-aged (30-50) recreational athletes ("weekend warriors") during sudden push-off activities. Risk factors include fluoroquinolone use, corticosteroid injections, and pre-existing tendinopathy.')
B.ST=s(["Dorsiflexing the ankle against resistance","Squeezing the calf and observing for plantarflexion","Palpating the tendon for a gap","Having the patient walk on toes"],t.s)
B.a91=new A.e("The Thompson test for Achilles tendon rupture is performed by:",B.ST,1,"The Thompson test: patient prone, squeeze the calf \u2014 normal response is passive plantarflexion. If the foot does not plantarflex (positive Thompson), the Achilles tendon is likely ruptured. It is the most reliable clinical test for complete rupture.")
B.Zk=s(["Posteriorly behind the lateral malleolus","Anteriorly over the lateral malleolus","Medially under the medial malleolus","Superiorly above the ankle joint"],t.s)
B.aaa=new A.e("Peroneal tendon subluxation most commonly occurs in which direction?",B.Zk,1,"Peroneal tendons sublux anteriorly over the lateral malleolus when the superior peroneal retinaculum is torn. It occurs with forceful dorsiflexion and eversion, commonly in skiing. Patients feel a snapping sensation at the lateral ankle.")
B.U7=s(["Weber classification","Lauge-Hansen classification","Grade I-III severity system","Danis-Weber classification"],t.s)
B.a5e=new A.e("Which classification system is used for lateral ankle sprains?",B.U7,2,"Lateral ankle sprains use Grade I (stretch/microscopic tear, mild swelling), Grade II (partial tear, moderate swelling, some instability), Grade III (complete tear, significant swelling, gross instability). Weber and Lauge-Hansen classify ankle fractures, not sprains.")
B.Ol=s(["Deltoid ligament","Spring ligament","Anterior talofibular ligament","Calcaneofibular ligament"],t.s)
B.a5j=new A.e("Chronic lateral ankle instability most commonly results from repeated injury to which ligament?",B.Ol,2,"Chronic lateral ankle instability most commonly results from repeated ATFL injuries that fail to heal properly. Up to 20% of acute ankle sprains progress to chronic instability. The Brostr\xf6m procedure (direct ligament repair) is the surgical treatment of choice.")
B.SF=s([B.ad6,B.a91,B.aaa,B.a5e,B.a5j],t.b)
B.J8=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Ankle 2",B.SF)
B.P5=s(["Yes, any vegetative state beyond 3 months is permanent","Yes, 6 months exceeds the threshold for traumatic injuries","No, permanent vegetative state after TBI requires 12 months","No, permanent vegetative state cannot be declared for traumatic injuries"],t.s)
B.a4b=new A.e("A patient in a vegetative state after a traumatic brain injury has been in that state for 6 months. Can this be classified as permanent?",B.P5,2,"For traumatic brain injury, a vegetative state is not considered permanent until 12 months have passed. For non-traumatic injuries such as anoxia, the threshold is 3 months.")
B.U0=s(["Spontaneous eye opening","Sleep-wake cycles on EEG","Visual fixation and smooth pursuit tracking","Roving eye movements"],t.s)
B.ab3=new A.e("Which finding best distinguishes a minimally conscious state from a vegetative state?",B.U0,2,"Visual fixation and smooth pursuit tracking are major differentiators between MCS and VS. VS patients have roving eye movements but cannot lock on and follow an object.")
B.Yo=s(["Any movement of the extremities in response to pain","The hand crosses the midline toward the stimulus to stop it","Flexion posturing of the upper extremities","Extension posturing of all extremities"],t.s)
B.ac1=new A.e("On the Glasgow Coma Scale motor subscale, what distinguishes localization (score of 5) from withdrawal (score of 4)?",B.Yo,1,"Localization requires the hand to cross the midline and move toward the stimulus in a coordinated attempt to stop it, indicating higher-level brain processing than simple withdrawal.")
B.XW=s(["Bromocriptine","Methylphenidate","Amantadine","Modafinil"],t.s)
B.a47=new A.e("Which pharmacologic agent has class one evidence from the NEJM for accelerating functional recovery in severe TBI disorders of consciousness?",B.XW,2,"Amantadine is the only agent with strong class one evidence, from a major NEJM paper by Giacino in 2012, showing it accelerated functional recovery in severe TBI.")
B.Uy=s(["Internal capsule","Thalamus","Red nucleus","Cerebral cortex"],t.s)
B.a4m=new A.e("In decerebrate posturing, the lesion is located at or below which anatomical structure?",B.Uy,2,"Decerebrate posturing results from a lesion at or below the red nucleus in the midbrain. With the red nucleus knocked out, the vestibulospinal tract drives unopposed extension of all extremities.")
B.RR=s([B.a4b,B.ab3,B.ac1,B.a47,B.a4m],t.b)
B.J9=new A.a8("Traumatic Brain Injury 2",B.RR)
B.Pg=s(["Upper extremity","Lower extremity","Trunk","Neck"],t.s)
B.a5z=new A.e("Common peroneal neuropathy at the fibular head is the most common entrapment neuropathy in which region?",B.Pg,1,"Common peroneal neuropathy at the fibular head is the most common lower extremity entrapment neuropathy. It causes foot drop with a steppage gait pattern. Risk factors include leg crossing, prolonged bed rest, weight loss, and fibular head fractures.")
B.Qi=s(["Metabolic damage from chronic hyperglycemia","Immune-mediated vasculitis of the vasa nervorum","Direct glucose toxicity to motor neurons","Compression from diabetic lipohypertrophy"],t.s)
B.aaD=new A.e("Diabetic amyotrophy (diabetic lumbosacral radiculoplexus neuropathy) is caused by:",B.Qi,1,"Diabetic amyotrophy (DLRPN) is NOT metabolic \u2014 it is immune-mediated vasculitis of the vasa nervorum. It causes severe pain followed by proximal weakness, often after weight loss or improved glycemic control. It is multifocal, asymmetric, and can be bilateral.")
B.a_l=s(["Diabetic peripheral neuropathy","Carpal tunnel syndrome","Vasculitis","Hereditary neuropathy"],t.s)
B.a9V=new A.e("A mononeuritis multiplex pattern on NCS (multifocal, asymmetric, patchy nerve involvement) is most suggestive of:",B.a_l,2,"Mononeuritis multiplex (multifocal, asymmetric, patchy nerve involvement) is the hallmark pattern of vasculitis affecting the vasa nervorum. Each nerve is hit randomly as its individual blood supply is attacked. This pattern demands workup for systemic vasculitis.")
B.PD=s(["Circumducting the leg in an arc","Dropping the opposite hip during stance","Exaggerated knee flexion to clear the dropping foot","Waddling side to side"],t.s)
B.acV=new A.e("Foot drop with a steppage gait is characterized by:",B.PD,2,"Steppage gait: the patient lifts the knee higher than normal during swing phase to clear the dropping foot from the ground. It results from ankle dorsiflexor weakness (peroneal nerve or L5 root). Circumduction = UMN/hip weakness. Trendelenburg = gluteus medius weakness.")
B.ZF=s(["It is the largest sensory nerve in the body","It is easily accessible and provides a reliable lower extremity SNAP","It is purely motor","It innervates the entire foot"],t.s)
B.a6h=new A.e("The sural nerve is most useful in NCS because:",B.ZF,1,"The sural nerve (terminal sensory branch of the tibial nerve) is easily accessible at the mid-calf behind the lateral malleolus. It provides a reliable lower extremity SNAP and is routinely tested in neuropathy evaluations. Its absence strongly suggests a generalized neuropathy.")
B.MB=s([B.a5z,B.aaD,B.a9V,B.acV,B.a6h],t.b)
B.Ja=new A.a8("Electrodiagnostic 11",B.MB)
B.ZR=s(["Foveal artery (ligamentum teres)","Medial femoral circumflex artery","Obturator artery","Superior gluteal artery"],t.s)
B.a86=new A.e("The blood supply to the femoral head primarily comes from the:",B.ZR,1,"The medial femoral circumflex artery provides the primary blood supply to the femoral head via the retinacular vessels. Femoral neck fractures can disrupt this supply, causing AVN. The foveal artery (via ligamentum teres) provides only a small contribution.")
B.VV=s(["Iliopsoas","Gluteus maximus","Gluteus medius","Quadriceps femoris"],t.s)
B.a9v=new A.e("A positive Trendelenburg sign indicates weakness of which muscle?",B.VV,2,"A positive Trendelenburg sign occurs when the pelvis drops on the unsupported side during single-leg stance, indicating gluteus medius weakness on the stance side. The gluteus medius (L5, superior gluteal nerve) is the primary hip abductor that stabilizes the pelvis during gait.")
B.WJ=s(["Incomplete/valgus impacted fracture","Complete but non-displaced fracture","Complete with partial displacement","Complete fracture with full displacement"],t.s)
B.aa4=new A.e("Femoral neck fractures are classified using the Garden classification. Garden Type IV indicates:",B.WJ,3,"Garden classification: Type I = incomplete/impacted (good prognosis). Type II = complete, non-displaced. Type III = complete, partially displaced. Type IV = completely displaced (highest AVN risk). Types III and IV typically require arthroplasty rather than fixation.")
B.Wy=s(["Hip extension and external rotation","Hip flexion >90\xb0, adduction, and internal rotation","Hip abduction and external rotation","Hip flexion <45\xb0 and abduction"],t.s)
B.ada=new A.e("After total hip arthroplasty with a posterior approach, which movement is restricted to prevent dislocation?",B.Wy,1,"Posterior approach hip precautions: avoid flexion >90\xb0, adduction past midline, and internal rotation. These positions stress the posterior capsule repair. An elevated toilet seat, hip abduction pillow, and avoiding low chairs are standard precautions.")
B.QL=s(["Diabetes mellitus","Corticosteroid use","Smoking","Osteoarthritis"],t.s)
B.a7l=new A.e("Avascular necrosis of the femoral head is most commonly associated with which risk factor?",B.QL,1,"Corticosteroid use is the most common non-traumatic cause of femoral head AVN. Other risk factors include alcohol abuse, sickle cell disease, systemic lupus, and femoral neck fractures. Steroid-induced AVN is thought to result from fat emboli and increased intraosseous pressure.")
B.SX=s([B.a86,B.a9v,B.aa4,B.ada,B.a7l],t.b)
B.Jb=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Hip 1",B.SX)
B.Rv=s(["10 mmHg","20 mmHg","30 mmHg (or within 30 mmHg of diastolic pressure)","50 mmHg"],t.s)
B.ade=new A.e("Acute compartment syndrome requires emergent fasciotomy when compartment pressure exceeds:",B.Rv,2,"Compartment syndrome requires fasciotomy when pressure >30 mmHg or within 30 mmHg of diastolic (delta P <30). The 5 P's (pain out of proportion, pain with passive stretch, paresthesias, pallor, pulselessness) are classic but pulselessness is a late and ominous sign.")
B.X9=s(["Pulselessness","Paralysis","Pain with passive stretch of the affected compartment muscles","Pallor"],t.s)
B.a59=new A.e("The most reliable early clinical finding of acute compartment syndrome is:",B.X9,2,"Pain with passive stretch of the muscles in the affected compartment is the earliest and most reliable clinical sign. For the anterior compartment, passively plantarflexing the foot stretches the tibialis anterior. Waiting for pulselessness means irreversible damage has occurred.")
B.OI=s(["Anterior compartment syndrome","Stress reaction at the posteromedial tibial cortex","Achilles tendon rupture","Peroneal nerve entrapment"],t.s)
B.a7N=new A.e("Medial tibial stress syndrome (shin splints) involves:",B.OI,1,"Medial tibial stress syndrome is a stress reaction/periostitis at the posteromedial tibial border, typically in the distal two-thirds. It is caused by repetitive loading (running on hard surfaces). It exists on a continuum with tibial stress fractures.")
B.NZ=s(["Gastrocnemius and soleus","Tibialis posterior and FDL","Tibialis anterior, EHL, EDL, and peroneus tertius","Peroneus longus and brevis"],t.s)
B.a4y=new A.e("The anterior compartment of the leg contains which muscles?",B.NZ,2,"The anterior compartment (deep peroneal nerve, anterior tibial artery) contains: tibialis anterior (dorsiflexion/inversion), EHL (great toe extension), EDL (toe extension), and peroneus tertius (dorsiflexion/eversion). Injury causes foot drop.")
B.Re=s(["It requires emergent fasciotomy","It causes irreversible muscle necrosis","Symptoms resolve with rest and recur with exercise","It only affects the posterior compartment"],t.s)
B.aax=new A.e("Chronic exertional compartment syndrome differs from acute compartment syndrome in that:",B.Re,2,"Chronic exertional compartment syndrome causes exercise-induced pain that resolves with rest (usually within 15-30 minutes). It does not cause permanent damage. Diagnosis requires pre- and post-exercise compartment pressure measurements. Fasciotomy is curative for refractory cases.")
B.RP=s([B.ade,B.a59,B.a7N,B.a4y,B.aax],t.b)
B.Jc=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Lower Leg",B.RP)
B.T6=s(["ACL deficiency","Weak vastus medialis obliquus (VMO)","Hamstring overactivity","Lateral meniscus tear"],t.s)
B.a6j=new A.e("Patellofemoral pain syndrome is the most common cause of anterior knee pain. Which of the following is a key contributing factor?",B.T6,1,"VMO weakness allows the patella to track laterally, increasing patellofemoral contact pressures. VMO strengthening (especially terminal knee extension and isometric quad sets) is central to treatment. Other factors include IT band tightness, increased Q-angle, and femoral anteversion.")
B.Wn=s(["MCL instability","Lateral patellar tracking and patellofemoral pain","ACL deficiency","Posterior knee pain"],t.s)
B.a6C=new A.e("The Q-angle is measured from the ASIS to the center of the patella to the tibial tubercle. An increased Q-angle is associated with:",B.Wn,1,"An increased Q-angle (normal: ~14\xb0 males, ~17\xb0 females) creates a lateral vector force on the patella during quadriceps contraction, promoting lateral tracking and patellofemoral pain. Women have larger Q-angles due to wider pelves.")
B.VL=s(["Inferior pole of the patella","Tibial tubercle apophysis","Femoral condyle","Popliteal fossa"],t.s)
B.ab_=new A.e("Osgood-Schlatter disease involves inflammation at which location?",B.VL,1,"Osgood-Schlatter is a traction apophysitis at the tibial tubercle where the patellar tendon inserts. It occurs in active adolescents during growth spurts. Sinding-Larsen-Johansson is the counterpart at the inferior pole of the patella.")
B.UL=s(["Infrapatellar bursa","Semimembranosus-gastrocnemius bursa (knee joint capsule)","Pes anserine bursa","Prepatellar bursa"],t.s)
B.a7V=new A.e("Baker's cyst (popliteal cyst) is a fluid collection that communicates with which structure?",B.UL,1,"Baker's cysts arise from the semimembranosus-gastrocnemius bursa, which communicates with the knee joint through a one-way valve. They are usually secondary to intra-articular pathology (meniscal tears, arthritis). Rupture can mimic DVT (pseudothrombophlebitis).")
B.Yy=s(["Lateral knee","Posterior knee","Medial knee, approximately 2 inches below the joint line","Anterior knee at the tibial tubercle"],t.s)
B.abQ=new A.e("Pes anserine bursitis causes pain at which location?",B.Yy,2,'The pes anserinus ("goose foot") is the conjoined tendon insertion of the sartorius, gracilis, and semitendinosus on the medial proximal tibia, ~2 inches below the joint line. Bursitis causes medial knee pain, especially with stair climbing. Common in obese women with knee OA.')
B.NV=s([B.a6j,B.a6C,B.ab_,B.a7V,B.abQ],t.b)
B.Jd=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Knee 2",B.NV)
B.Os=s(["One inch","Two inches","Three inches","Half an inch"],t.s)
B.acN=new A.e("In SCIWORA, the pediatric spine can stretch up to how many inches without disrupting, while the spinal cord can only tolerate about a quarter inch?",B.Os,1,"The pediatric vertebral column can stretch up to two inches without disrupting due to its fibrocartilaginous nature, while the spinal cord can only stretch about a quarter inch before damage occurs.")
B.Ts=s(["Paraplegia","Tetraplegia","Hemiplegia","Monoplegia"],t.s)
B.a5N=new A.e("What replaced the term quadriplegia in 1992 for linguistic consistency?",B.Ts,1,"Tetraplegia replaced quadriplegia in 1992 because quadri is Latin and plegia is Greek; tetra is also Greek, making tetraplegia linguistically consistent.")
B.XB=s(["Flexion injury from a fall","Hyperextension of the neck causing a pincer effect","Axial loading from a fall on the head","Rotational injury from a motor vehicle crash"],t.s)
B.abX=new A.e("In elderly patients with SCIWORA, what mechanism typically causes the injury?",B.XB,1,"In elderly patients, SCIWORA typically results from hyperextension of the neck during a fall, where the ligamentum flavum buckles inward and pinches the cord against anterior osteophytes.")
B.YP=s(["Bone scan","Myelography","MRI","PET scan"],t.s)
B.a8_=new A.e("What is the gold standard imaging modality for diagnosing SCIWORA when CT and X-ray are normal?",B.YP,2,"MRI is the gold standard for SCIWORA because it can detect soft tissue pathology, ligamentous injuries, disc herniations, and signal changes in the cord itself that CT and X-ray cannot see.")
B.ZP=s(["Central cord lesions","Brachial plexus injuries or peripheral nerve injuries outside the neural canal","Incomplete cervical injuries","Brown-Sequard syndrome"],t.s)
B.abV=new A.e("What does the definition of tetraplegia specifically exclude, even if the arms are paralyzed?",B.ZP,1,"Tetraplegia refers strictly to impairment from cervical spinal cord damage within the neural canal. Brachial plexus injuries or peripheral nerve injuries outside the canal are excluded from the definition.")
B.RO=s([B.acN,B.a5N,B.abX,B.a8_,B.abV],t.b)
B.Je=new A.a8("Spinal Cord Injury 3",B.RO)
B.VF=s(["2 centimeters","4 centimeters","6 centimeters","8 centimeters"],t.s)
B.a4M=new A.e("An external locking elbow is used when the residual limb extends more distally than what distance to the epicondyles?",B.VF,1,"An external locking elbow is used when the residual limb extends more distally than 4 centimeters to the epicondyles.")
B.P0=s(["Flexion, adduction, internal rotation","Depression, extension, abduction","Elevation, flexion, adduction","Protraction, flexion, internal rotation"],t.s)
B.a5T=new A.e("To lock a transhumeral prosthetic elbow, the patient performs which shoulder movements?",B.P0,1,"The mnemonic: down, back, and out \u2014 shoulder depression, extension, and abduction.")
B.Xs=s(["50%","60%","70%","80%"],t.s)
B.a80=new A.e("What percentage of transhumeral amputees have limited humeral rotation?",B.Xs,3,"80% of transhumeral amputees have limited humeral rotation, making aggressive ROM therapy critical before prosthetic fitting.")
B.X5=s(["0.50 to 0.80","0.70 to 1.00","0.91 to 1.30","1.00 to 1.50"],t.s)
B.aag=new A.e("What is the normal ankle brachial index (ABI) range?",B.X5,2,"A normal ABI is 0.91 to 1.30 \u2014 ankle pressure roughly the same as or slightly higher than arm pressure.")
B.ME=s(["18 months","2 years","3 years","5 years"],t.s)
B.aaF=new A.e("At what age is dual-control body-powered transhumeral prosthetic training first introduced to children?",B.ME,2,"Dual-control training should not start before age 3 due to the cognitive complexity of the control sequences.")
B.RJ=s([B.a4M,B.a5T,B.a80,B.aag,B.aaF],t.b)
B.Jf=new A.a8("Prosthetics & Orthotics 3",B.RJ)
B.UE=s(["3 hours","4.5 hours","6 hours","12 hours"],t.s)
B.a6a=new A.e("The window for IV tPA administration in acute ischemic stroke is within how many hours of symptom onset?",B.UE,1,"IV tPA (alteplase) can be administered within 4.5 hours of symptom onset in eligible patients. The earlier it is given, the better the outcomes. Mechanical thrombectomy may extend the window to 24 hours for large vessel occlusions.")
B.Rs=s(["Glasgow Coma Scale","NIH Stroke Scale (NIHSS)","Modified Rankin Scale","Barthel Index"],t.s)
B.a7M=new A.e("Which standardized assessment tool is most commonly used to quantify stroke severity and guide treatment decisions?",B.Rs,1,"The NIH Stroke Scale (NIHSS) is the standard tool for quantifying stroke severity acutely. It scores 0-42, assessing consciousness, gaze, visual fields, facial palsy, motor function, ataxia, sensory, language, dysarthria, and extinction/inattention.")
B.Zb=s(["140/90 mmHg","160/100 mmHg","180/105 mmHg","220/120 mmHg"],t.s)
B.a7B=new A.e("Blood pressure management in the first 24 hours after tPA administration requires maintaining BP below which threshold?",B.Zb,2,"After tPA administration, blood pressure must be maintained below 180/105 mmHg to reduce the risk of hemorrhagic transformation. In patients NOT receiving tPA, permissive hypertension up to 220/120 is generally allowed.")
B.Vv=s(["Immediate aggressive blood pressure lowering","Permissive hypertension \u2014 do not treat","Lower blood pressure to 140/90","Administer tPA"],t.s)
B.a7S=new A.e("A patient with acute ischemic stroke who did NOT receive tPA has a blood pressure of 200/110 mmHg. What is the appropriate management?",B.Vv,1,"In patients with acute ischemic stroke who did not receive tPA, permissive hypertension is allowed up to 220/120 mmHg. The elevated BP helps maintain cerebral perfusion to the ischemic penumbra. Only treat if BP exceeds 220/120.")
B.a_2=s(["Barthel Index","FIM (Functional Independence Measure)","Modified Rankin Scale (mRS)","NIH Stroke Scale"],t.s)
B.a7i=new A.e("Which functional outcome measure classifies disability on a scale of 0 (no symptoms) to 6 (death) and is the primary endpoint in most stroke trials?",B.a_2,2,"The Modified Rankin Scale (mRS) is the primary outcome measure in most stroke clinical trials. It ranges from 0 (no symptoms) to 6 (death), with scores of 0-2 generally considered a favorable outcome.")
B.a_t=s([B.a6a,B.a7M,B.a7B,B.a7S,B.a7i],t.b)
B.Jg=new A.a8("Cerebrovascular Accident 4",B.a_t)
B.a_5=s(["A violation of patient autonomy","An exercise of patient autonomy through delegation","Evidence of lack of decisional capacity","Paternalism by the physician"],t.s)
B.a84=new A.e("A patient with decisional capacity voluntarily asks their physician to make treatment decisions on their behalf. Which of the following best describes this situation?",B.a_5,1,"Autonomy includes the right to delegate decision-making. If a patient voluntarily and with understanding chooses to have someone else decide, that delegation itself is an exercise of autonomy, not a violation of it.")
B.Zy=s(["Decisional capacity","Competence","Informed consent","Beneficence"],t.s)
B.a4F=new A.e("Which of the following is determined by a judge, not a physician?",B.Zy,1,"Competence is a legal determination made by a judge. Physicians assess decisional capacity, which is a clinical assessment that is task-specific and time-dependent.")
B.R_=s(["Justice and non-maleficence","Beneficence and autonomy","Non-maleficence and justice","Autonomy and competence"],t.s)
B.a8t=new A.e("A post-stroke patient with dysphagia is recommended a PEG tube but refuses, preferring oral intake despite aspiration risk. The physician respects this decision. Which ethical principles are in conflict?",B.R_,1,"The PEG tube recommendation represents beneficence (preventing aspiration pneumonia), while the patient's refusal represents autonomy. In modern bioethics, autonomy generally prevails when the patient has capacity.")
B.UO=s(["Non-comparative justice","Distributive justice","Comparative justice","Procedural justice"],t.s)
B.a5_=new A.e("An inpatient rehab unit has one bed remaining and two consults: a 25-year-old with TBI and high recovery potential, and a 95-year-old with end-stage dementia unlikely to make functional gains. Allocating the bed based on recovery potential is an example of which type of justice?",B.UO,2,"Comparative justice involves weighing patients against each other based on need and potential benefit. Non-comparative justice applies equal rules regardless of individual need (e.g., insurance caps on therapy visits).")
B.Si=s(["Patient competence or capacity","Voluntary decision-making","Written documentation signed by the patient","Disclosure of important information"],t.s)
B.a6L=new A.e("Which of the following is NOT one of the four elements required for valid informed consent?",B.Si,2,"The four elements of informed consent are: (1) patient competence/capacity, (2) voluntary decision-making, (3) disclosure of important information, and (4) authorization to proceed. Written documentation is good practice but not one of the four required elements.")
B.ZW=s([B.a84,B.a4F,B.a8t,B.a5_,B.a6L],t.b)
B.Jh=new A.a8("Bioethics",B.ZW)
B.ZG=s(["95% of those who test positive truly have the disease","95% of those who have the disease will test positive","5% of healthy individuals will test positive","95% of all test results are correct"],t.s)
B.a8K=new A.e("A screening test has 95% sensitivity. This means:",B.ZG,1,"Sensitivity = true positive rate = proportion of people WITH the disease who test positive. A sensitivity of 95% means the test catches 95% of true cases. High sensitivity is important for screening (SnNOut: Sensitive test, Negative result, rules Out disease).")
B.TJ=s(["Screening large populations","Ruling out disease when negative","Confirming disease when positive","Estimating disease prevalence"],t.s)
B.a58=new A.e("A test with high specificity is most useful for:",B.TJ,2,"High specificity means few false positives (SpPIn: Specific test, Positive result, rules In disease). Specific tests are best for confirming a diagnosis. Sensitive tests are best for screening/ruling out disease.")
B.XR=s(["You fail to reject a false null hypothesis","You reject a true null hypothesis","The sample size is too small","The confidence interval is too wide"],t.s)
B.a96=new A.e("A Type I error (alpha error) occurs when:",B.XR,1,"Type I error (alpha/false positive) = rejecting a true null hypothesis (finding a difference when none exists). Type II error (beta/false negative) = failing to reject a false null hypothesis (missing a real difference). Standard alpha = 0.05.")
B.Yf=s(["The probability of a Type I error","The probability of finding a difference when one truly exists","The size of the treatment effect","The confidence level of the study"],t.s)
B.a4X=new A.e("Power of a study is defined as:",B.Yf,1,"Power = 1 - beta = the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis (detecting a real effect). Standard power is 0.80 (80%). Power is increased by: larger sample size, larger effect size, and higher alpha level.")
B.Vz=s(["Excluded from the analysis","Analyzed as part of the treatment group regardless","Moved to the control group","Analyzed separately"],t.s)
B.a8d=new A.e("In an intention-to-treat analysis, patients who drop out of the treatment group are:",B.Vz,1,"Intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis keeps all randomized participants in their original assigned groups regardless of compliance, withdrawal, or crossover. It preserves the benefits of randomization and provides a more conservative, real-world estimate of treatment effect.")
B.Qv=s([B.a8K,B.a58,B.a96,B.a4X,B.a8d],t.b)
B.Ji=new A.a8("Biostatistics",B.Qv)
B.XN=s(["Posterior tibial translation","Anterior tibial translation","Valgus stress","Varus stress"],t.s)
B.a40=new A.e("The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) primarily resists:",B.XN,1,"The ACL prevents anterior translation of the tibia on the femur. It is tested with the Lachman test (most sensitive) and anterior drawer test. ACL tears are common in cutting/pivoting sports and have a female predominance (3-6x higher risk).")
B.Wt=s(["It tests at full extension","At 20-30\xb0 flexion, the hamstrings are relaxed and can't mask laxity","It tests the posterior cruciate ligament simultaneously","It requires less examiner skill"],t.s)
B.a5n=new A.e("The Lachman test is considered more sensitive than the anterior drawer test for ACL tears because:",B.Wt,1,"The Lachman test is performed at 20-30\xb0 flexion where hamstring guarding is minimized. The anterior drawer (90\xb0 flexion) allows the hamstrings to act as ACL synergists, potentially masking a tear. Lachman sensitivity is ~85-95% vs ~60-70% for anterior drawer.")
B.PQ=s(["ACL, LCL, and lateral meniscus","ACL, MCL, and medial meniscus","PCL, MCL, and lateral meniscus","ACL, PCL, and medial meniscus"],t.s)
B.a4z=new A.e('The "unhappy triad" (O\'Donoghue triad) involves injury to which three structures?',B.PQ,1,"The classic unhappy triad: ACL tear + MCL tear + medial meniscus tear. It results from a valgus force with external rotation (e.g., a lateral blow to the knee). Note: some modern literature shows the lateral meniscus may be more commonly injured than medial.")
B.Pk=s(["ACL","PCL","Meniscus","MCL"],t.s)
B.a7r=new A.e("The McMurray test evaluates which knee structure?",B.Pk,2,"McMurray's test: with the knee flexed, apply valgus + external rotation (medial meniscus) or varus + internal rotation (lateral meniscus) while extending. A pop or click with pain = positive for meniscal tear.")
B.QZ=s(["Lachman test","Anterior drawer test","Posterior drawer test / posterior sag sign","Pivot shift test"],t.s)
B.a5f=new A.e("The PCL is best tested with which maneuver?",B.QZ,2,"The posterior drawer test: at 90\xb0 flexion, push the tibia posteriorly. PCL tears also show a posterior sag sign \u2014 with knees flexed at 90\xb0, the tibial plateau sags posteriorly compared to the uninjured side. Dashboard injuries (tibia pushed back) are the classic mechanism.")
B.P_=s([B.a40,B.a5n,B.a4z,B.a7r,B.a5f],t.b)
B.Jj=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Knee 1",B.P_)
B.MQ=s(["At Guyon's canal","5-8 cm proximal to the wrist","At the cubital tunnel","At the axilla"],t.s)
B.aaX=new A.e("The dorsal ulnar cutaneous (DUC) nerve branches from the ulnar nerve at what location?",B.MQ,1,"The DUC branches 5-8 cm proximal to the wrist and does NOT travel through Guyon's canal. This is a critical landmark: DUC abnormal = lesion at or above the elbow. DUC normal = lesion at wrist (Guyon's canal).")
B.UT=s(["First dorsal interosseous","Adductor pollicis","Opponens pollicis","Flexor pollicis longus"],t.s)
B.a4l=new A.e("Froment's sign tests for weakness of which muscle?",B.UT,1,"Froment's sign: patient tries to hold paper between thumb and index finger. The adductor pollicis (ulnar nerve) is weak, so the patient compensates by flexing the thumb IP joint using FPL (median nerve). IP flexion during pinch = positive Froment sign = ulnar neuropathy.")
B.Xe=s(["Radiation causes fasciculations; tumor causes fibrillations","Radiation shows myokymic discharges; tumor is typically painful with Horner syndrome","They cannot be differentiated on EMG","Radiation affects lower trunk; tumor affects upper trunk"],t.s)
B.ack=new A.e("How do you differentiate radiation-induced brachial plexopathy from tumor recurrence on EMG?",B.Xe,1,"Key differentiators: Radiation plexopathy = painless, upper trunk predominant, myokymic discharges (marching soldiers on EMG). Tumor recurrence = painful, lower trunk predominant, may have Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhydrosis). Myokymia is the EMG key.")
B.PM=s(["Thumb and index finger","Index and middle fingers","Ring and little fingers","All five digits equally"],t.s)
B.a7v=new A.e("Ulnar claw hand affects primarily which digits?",B.PM,2,"Ulnar claw hand primarily affects the 4th and 5th digits. The unopposed EDC hyperextends the MCPs, and passive tension on the flexor tendons curls the PIP/DIP joints. The index and middle fingers are relatively spared because their lumbricals are median-innervated.")
B.MH=s(["Inability to cross the fingers","The small finger drifting into abduction at rest due to weak interossei","Weakness of thumb opposition","Inability to extend the wrist"],t.s)
B.a6T=new A.e("Wartenberg's sign is demonstrated by:",B.MH,1,"Wartenberg's sign: the small finger drifts into abduction because the third palmar interosseous (ulnar nerve) is weak and cannot adduct the 5th digit against the unopposed action of EDM. It is a subtle early sign of ulnar neuropathy.")
B.Pb=s([B.aaX,B.a4l,B.ack,B.a7v,B.a6T],t.b)
B.Jk=new A.a8("Electrodiagnostic 9",B.Pb)
B.RM=s(["10 times per second","25 times per second","50 times per second","100 times per second"],t.s)
B.a5o=new A.e("How often does a microprocessor-controlled knee recalibrate its stability settings?",B.RM,2,"The microprocessor knee recalibrates 50 times per second (50 Hz), constantly reading sensors to prevent falls.")
B.Yz=s(["Contralateral ischial tuberosity","Ipsilateral ischial tuberosity","Soft tissues of the amputated side","Greater trochanter"],t.s)
B.abP=new A.e("In a hip disarticulation prosthesis, where is the primary weight bearing?",B.Yz,1,"Weight bearing is on the ipsilateral (amputated side) ischial tuberosity in hip disarticulation.")
B.OC=s(["Socket is too loose throughout","Proximal socket is too tight while distal end lacks total contact","Socket is too short","Liner causes allergic reaction"],t.s)
B.ac4=new A.e("What is the underlying mechanism of choke syndrome in a prosthetic socket?",B.OC,1,"A tight proximal socket creates a tourniquet effect while the loose distal end traps fluid, causing edema and verrucous hyperplasia.")
B.a_x=s(["10-25%","25-40%","50-85%","90-100%"],t.s)
B.ab0=new A.e("What percentage of amputees experience phantom limb pain?",B.a_x,2,"50-85% of amputees experience phantom limb pain, though chronic disabling pain lasting beyond 6 months is rare (<5%).")
B.Wi=s(["Hydraulic units are lighter","Pneumatic fluid (air) is compressible while hydraulic fluid (oil) is incompressible","Pneumatic handles more load","Hydraulic units are less expensive"],t.s)
B.a7g=new A.e("What distinguishes hydraulic from pneumatic prosthetic knee units?",B.Wi,1,"Pneumatic uses compressible air (lighter, less load); hydraulic uses incompressible oil (heavier, handles more force).")
