B.Jm=new A.a8("Cerebrovascular Accident 5",B.Z3)
B.VD=s(["Loss of consciousness is required for diagnosis","Post-traumatic amnesia must exceed 24 hours","An alteration in mental status at the time of injury alone can meet the diagnostic criteria","A GCS of 12 is consistent with a mild TBI classification"],t.s)
B.aa5=new A.e("According to the ACRM criteria for mild TBI, which of the following is true?",B.VD,2,"The ACRM criteria require at least one of four manifestations: any loss of consciousness, any amnesia before or after the event, any alteration in mental status at the time of injury, or any focal neurologic deficit. Loss of consciousness is not required.")
B.Yj=s(["Less than 15 minutes","Less than 30 minutes","Less than 60 minutes","Less than 24 hours"],t.s)
B.a4O=new A.e("What is the upper limit of loss of consciousness duration that still qualifies as a mild TBI?",B.Yj,1,"For mild TBI, loss of consciousness must be less than 30 minutes. If the patient is unconscious for 31 minutes or more, the injury is classified as moderate TBI by definition.")
B.TQ=s(["Walking","Stationary cycling","Resistance training","Swimming"],t.s)
B.ace=new A.e("In the 2016 Berlin consensus graduated return-to-play protocol, which activity is specifically prohibited during step two (light aerobic exercise)?",B.TQ,2,"Step two allows light aerobic exercise such as walking, swimming, or stationary cycling with heart rate below 70% of max. However, resistance training is specifically prohibited at this stage.")
B.Ot=s(["Amantadine","Donepezil","Methylphenidate","CDP-choline"],t.s)
B.ab5=new A.e("Which pharmacologic agent is most associated with improving processing speed in TBI patients?",B.Ot,2,"The transcript specifically states that the key phrase to associate with methylphenidate and TBI is processing speed. While it helps with attention broadly, the literature specifically points to benefits in the speed of information processing.")
B.YB=s(["A delayed seizure occurring weeks after the first TBI","A catastrophic loss of cerebral vascular autoregulation after a second head injury sustained before recovery from the first, with mortality near 50%","A progressive decline in cognition seen after repeated subconcussive blows over years","A rebound increase in intracranial pressure after premature discontinuation of mannitol"],t.s)
B.acZ=new A.e("What is second impact syndrome and why is it so dangerous?",B.YB,1,"Second impact syndrome occurs when a still-symptomatic patient sustains a second head injury, triggering complete failure of cerebral vascular autoregulation, massive ICP elevation, brain herniation, and mortality close to 50% with near 100% morbidity in survivors.")
