B.a6o=new A.e("A patient has clinical sensory loss in a dermatomal distribution but normal SNAPs. The lesion is most likely:",B.Yh,1,"Pre-ganglionic lesions (radiculopathy) preserve the SNAP because the sensory neuron cell body in the DRG and its peripheral axon remain intact. Post-ganglionic lesions (plexopathy, peripheral neuropathy) damage the axon distal to the DRG, causing abnormal SNAPs.")
B.Ud=s(["25%","40%","50%","75%"],t.s)
B.acm=new A.e("Conduction block on NCS is defined as a drop in CMAP amplitude of greater than what percentage between distal and proximal stimulation sites?",B.Ud,2,"Conduction block is defined as >50% drop in CMAP amplitude between proximal and distal stimulation sites. It is a hallmark of focal demyelination and indicates that some axons fail to conduct past the lesion site.")
B.TW=s(["Grade 1 (neuropraxia)","Grade 2 (axonotmesis)","Grade 3","Grade 4"],t.s)
B.a9x=new A.e("In the Sunderland classification, which grade involves disruption of the endoneurium with intact perineurium?",B.TW,2,"Sunderland Grade 3 involves disruption of the endoneurium with intact perineurium. Grade 1 = neuropraxia (myelin only). Grade 2 = axonotmesis (axon damaged, endoneurium intact). Grade 4 = perineurium damaged. Grade 5 = complete transection (neurotmesis).")
B.Xp=s(["Axonal loss of large fibers","Different conduction velocities of demyelinated fibers","Neuromuscular junction block","Electrode placement errors"],t.s)
B.a8u=new A.e("Temporal dispersion on NCS is caused by:",B.Xp,1,"Temporal dispersion occurs when different fibers conduct at different speeds due to variable degrees of demyelination. Fast and slow fibers arrive at the recording electrode at different times, spreading out and reducing the CMAP amplitude with a widened, polyphasic waveform.")
B.TC=s(["Axonal: slow velocity, normal amplitude","Demyelinating: low amplitude, normal velocity","Axonal: low amplitude, normal velocity","Both show identical findings"],t.s)
