B.Rh=s(["Hip flexors","Knee extensors","Neck flexors","Shoulder abductors"],t.s)
B.abL=new A.e("In Duchenne muscular dystrophy, what is the earliest detectable area of weakness?",B.Rh,2,"The earliest detectable sign of weakness in DMD is in the neck flexors, appearing in the preschool years.")
B.S5=s(["Age 5","Age 10","Age 15","Age 20"],t.s)
B.a41=new A.e("What is the average age for wheelchair dependency in Duchenne muscular dystrophy?",B.S5,1,"The average age for becoming wheelchair dependent is roughly 10 years old (range 7-13).")
B.ZQ=s(["Cardiac arrhythmias","Malignant hyperthermia with certain anesthetics","Respiratory failure during sleep","Progressive intellectual disability"],t.s)
B.aba=new A.e("What is the critical safety concern associated with central core myopathy?",B.ZQ,1,"Central core myopathy is tightly linked to malignant hyperthermia from inhaled anesthetics like sevoflurane or succinylcholine.")
B.Pp=s(["Calf hypertrophy, Gower sign, lordosis","Early contractures (elbows, ankles, spine), humero-peroneal weakness, cardiac conduction defects","Facial weakness, scapular winging, hearing loss","Myotonia, cataracts, frontal balding"],t.s)
B.ac2=new A.e("What is the clinical triad of Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy?",B.Pp,1,"The EMD triad: early contractures of elbows/ankles/spine, humero-peroneal weakness, and life-threatening cardiac conduction defects.")
B.UD=s(["Deltoid muscles","Biceps muscles","Extraocular and pharyngeal muscles","Scapular stabilizers"],t.s)
B.a4k=new A.e("In facioscapulohumeral dystrophy (FSHD), which muscles are characteristically spared?",B.UD,2,"In FSHD, extraocular and pharyngeal muscles are spared, distinguishing it from mitochondrial or oculopharyngeal dystrophy.")
B.MG=s([B.abL,B.a41,B.aba,B.ac2,B.a4k],t.b)
B.Js=new A.a8("Pediatrics 10",B.MG)
B.WR=s(["Hepatotoxicity","Seizures","Renal failure","Pulmonary embolism"],t.s)
B.a7Q=new A.e("Brain tumor patients undergoing rehabilitation should be monitored for which common complication?",B.WR,1,"Seizures are a very common complication in brain tumor patients (occurring in 20-40% of cases). Antiepileptic medications are frequently required. Therapy programs should be adjusted to ensure patient safety during activities.")
B.Pn=s(["0 to 5","0 to 10","0 to 100","1 to 7"],t.s)
B.a5E=new A.e("The Karnofsky Performance Status scale ranges from:",B.Pn,2,"The Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) scale ranges from 0 (dead) to 100 (normal, no complaints). It is widely used in oncology to assess functional status. A KPS of 70+ generally indicates ability to care for self; below 50 indicates need for significant assistance.")
B.Ub=s(["Increase risk of metastasis","Have no effect on fatigue","Reduce fatigue and improve quality of life","Be contraindicated during chemotherapy"],t.s)
B.acn=new A.e("Exercise during cancer treatment has been shown to:",B.Ub,2,"Exercise during cancer treatment is the best-studied intervention for cancer-related fatigue. It reduces fatigue, improves quality of life, maintains muscle mass, and may improve treatment tolerance. It is not contraindicated during chemotherapy in most patients.")
B.Ti=s(["Cervical spine","Thoracic spine","Lumbar spine","Sacral spine"],t.s)
B.a5J=new A.e("Spinal cord compression from metastatic cancer most commonly involves which region?",B.Ti,1,"Metastatic spinal cord compression most commonly occurs in the thoracic spine (approximately 70% of cases), followed by the lumbosacral spine, then cervical spine. This reflects the relative volume of the thoracic spine and common metastatic spread patterns.")
B.WC=s(["Lung, liver, pancreas, colon","Breast, prostate, lung, kidney, thyroid","Brain, ovarian, gastric, esophageal","Melanoma, sarcoma, lymphoma, leukemia"],t.s)
B.a6u=new A.e("Which cancers most commonly metastasize to bone?",B.WC,1,'The cancers most likely to metastasize to bone can be remembered with the mnemonic "BLT with a Kosher Pickle" \u2014 Breast, Lung, Thyroid, Kidney, Prostate. These account for the vast majority of skeletal metastases.')
B.Zo=s([B.a7Q,B.a5E,B.acn,B.a5J,B.a6u],t.b)
B.Jt=new A.a8("Cancer Rehab 3",B.Zo)
B.VA=s(["Deltoid ligament","Calcaneofibular ligament","Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL)","Posterior talofibular ligament"],t.s)
B.a8T=new A.e("The most commonly injured ligament in ankle sprains is:",B.VA,2,"The ATFL is the weakest lateral ankle ligament and the most commonly injured in inversion ankle sprains. It is taut in plantarflexion and resists anterior translation of the talus. The injury sequence is ATFL \u2192 CFL \u2192 PTFL.")
B.Pi=s(["Deltoid ligament","Calcaneofibular ligament","Anterior talofibular ligament","Syndesmosis"],t.s)
B.a7W=new A.e("The anterior drawer test of the ankle primarily assesses the integrity of which structure?",B.Pi,2,"The anterior drawer test assesses the ATFL by stabilizing the tibia and pulling the calcaneus/talus anteriorly. Excessive anterior translation compared to the uninjured side indicates ATFL insufficiency.")
B.Y_=s(["Anterior talofibular ligament","Achilles tendon insertion","Posterior edge of the distal 6 cm of the fibula (lateral malleolus)","Anterior tibialis tendon"],t.s)
B.a9D=new A.e("The Ottawa Ankle Rules state that ankle X-rays are indicated if there is bone tenderness at which of the following locations?",B.Y_,2,"Ottawa Ankle Rules indicate X-ray if there is: bone tenderness at the posterior edge of the distal 6 cm of the lateral malleolus OR medial malleolus, OR inability to bear weight for 4 steps immediately after injury and in the ED.")
B.Wv=s(["Anterior talofibular ligament","Deltoid ligament","Tibiofibular syndesmosis","Spring ligament"],t.s)
B.abi=new A.e("A high ankle sprain involves injury to which structure?",B.Wv,2,"A high ankle sprain involves the tibiofibular syndesmosis (the interosseous membrane and anterior/posterior tibiofibular ligaments connecting the distal tibia and fibula). It is more serious than a lateral ankle sprain and requires longer recovery. The squeeze test and external rotation test help diagnose it.")
B.a_9=s(["Lateral side; resists inversion","Medial side; resists eversion","Lateral side; resists eversion","Medial side; resists inversion"],t.s)
B.acp=new A.e("The deltoid ligament is located on which side of the ankle and resists which motion?",B.a_9,1,"The deltoid ligament is on the medial side of the ankle and is the strongest ankle ligament. It resists eversion. Deltoid injuries are less common than lateral ligament injuries because the deltoid is so strong \u2014 an eversion force is more likely to cause a fracture (especially fibula) than a deltoid tear.")
B.UY=s([B.a8T,B.a7W,B.a9D,B.abi,B.acp],t.b)
B.Ju=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Ankle 1",B.UY)
B.Q0=s(["2 or more","3 or more","5 or more","8 or more"],t.s)
B.a4B=new A.e("How many joints must be involved in the first six months to classify JIA as polyarticular?",B.Q0,2,"Polyarticular JIA requires five or more joints involved in the first six months.")
B.WV=s(["20 to 30%","40 to 50%","55 to 65%","70 to 80%"],t.s)
B.a7P=new A.e("What percentage of children with oligoarticular JIA have positive ANA titers, putting them at high risk for asymptomatic uveitis?",B.WV,3,"Positive ANA titers are found in 70 to 80% of oligoarticular JIA patients, correlating with high risk for asymptomatic uveitis.")
B.UA=s(["Butterfly rash across the cheeks","Evanescent salmon-colored rash","Violaceous heliotrope rash on the eyelids","Erythema migrans target lesion"],t.s)
B.a5O=new A.e("What is the hallmark rash of systemic JIA?",B.UA,1,"The classic evanescent salmon-colored rash appears when the fever spikes and disappears when the fever breaks.")
B.U6=s(["Radial deviation at the wrist, ulnar drift at the fingers","Ulnar deviation at the wrist, radial deviation at the fingers","Symmetric ulnar deviation at both wrist and fingers","No deviation pattern in children"],t.s)
B.a4v=new A.e("In children with JIA, the wrist deviation pattern is the reverse of adult RA. What pattern is seen in children?",B.U6,1,"Children develop ulnar deviation at the wrist and radial deviation at the MCP joints, the complete reverse of the adult RA pattern.")
B.Rt=s(["Septic arthritis","Macrophage activation syndrome (MAS)","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Hemolytic uremic syndrome"],t.s)
B.aaV=new A.e("Which life-threatening complication of systemic JIA is characterized by a paradoxical drop in ESR while the patient worsens?",B.Rt,1,"In MAS, the ESR paradoxically drops because fibrinogen is consumed, even as the patient gets sicker.")
B.Q2=s([B.a4B,B.a7P,B.a5O,B.a4v,B.aaV],t.b)
B.Jv=new A.a8("Pediatrics 4",B.Q2)
B.Uf=s(["Supraspinatus","Infraspinatus","Subscapularis","Teres minor"],t.s)
B.a6Z=new A.e("The lift-off test (Gerber test) evaluates which rotator cuff muscle?",B.Uf,2,"The lift-off test: hand placed behind the back (internally rotated), patient pushes away from the back. Inability to lift the hand off = subscapularis tear. The belly press test is an alternative for patients who cannot reach behind their back.")
B.Nh=s(["Infraspinatus","Subscapularis","Teres minor","Supraspinatus"],t.s)
B.a4Q=new A.e("Calcific tendinitis most commonly affects which rotator cuff tendon?",B.Nh,3,"Calcific tendinitis most commonly affects the supraspinatus tendon in its critical zone of relative hypovascularity. The resorptive phase is the most painful. Treatment ranges from NSAIDs and PT to needle aspiration or ultrasound-guided barbotage.")
B.OA=s(["Deltoid weakness and lateral arm numbness","Hand intrinsic weakness and medial forearm/hand numbness","Shoulder pain with overhead activities","Biceps weakness and lateral forearm numbness"],t.s)
B.abW=new A.e("Thoracic outlet syndrome (TOS) with lower trunk involvement (C8-T1) presents with:",B.OA,1,"Neurogenic TOS affecting the lower trunk (C8-T1) presents with hand intrinsic muscle weakness/atrophy and numbness in the medial forearm and hand (ulnar distribution). Upper trunk (C5-C6) involvement is less common and affects shoulder/arm.")
B.Sg=s(["Resisted shoulder internal rotation","Extending the neck, rotating toward the affected side, and taking a deep breath while palpating the radial pulse","Cross-body adduction of the arm","Overhead arm elevation for 3 minutes"],t.s)
B.a8z=new A.e("The Adson test for thoracic outlet syndrome involves:",B.Sg,1,"Adson's test: extend the neck, rotate toward the affected side, deep inspiration \u2014 palpate the radial pulse. Diminished pulse or reproduction of symptoms = positive. However, false positive rates are high (~50% of normals).")
B.Y9=s(["AC ligament sprain only","AC ligament tear with CC ligaments intact","Complete AC and CC ligament disruption with clavicle elevated 25-100%","Posterior dislocation of the clavicle"],t.s)
B.a8F=new A.e("In acromioclavicular joint separations, a Rockwood Type III involves:",B.Y9,2,"Rockwood classification: Type I = AC sprain. Type II = AC torn, CC intact. Type III = both AC and CC disrupted, clavicle elevated 25-100% (controversial for surgical vs conservative). Types IV-VI involve more severe displacement and usually require surgery.")
B.Ze=s([B.a6Z,B.a4Q,B.abW,B.a8z,B.a8F],t.b)
B.Jw=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Shoulder 4",B.Ze)
B.Sp=s(["MS fatigue is constant throughout the day","MS fatigue is typically better in the morning and worsens as the day progresses","MS fatigue improves with exercise","MS fatigue is worse in cold weather"],t.s)
B.acX=new A.e("What distinguishes MS fatigue from depression-related fatigue?",B.Sp,1,"MS fatigue is typically better in the morning (battery starts full) and worsens significantly as the day progresses. In depression, patients often feel worst upon waking with early morning awakening.")
B.ZH=s(["Presence of ptosis","Convergence is intact despite failed adduction on lateral gaze","Bilateral pupil dilation","Complete loss of all eye movements"],t.s)
B.abb=new A.e("In internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO), what confirms the diagnosis is an MLF lesion rather than a CN III palsy?",B.ZH,1,"In INO, convergence is intact because it uses a pathway that bypasses the MLF entirely, going from the pretectal area directly to CN III nuclei bilaterally. If convergence works but lateral gaze adduction fails, it confirms an MLF lesion.")
B.a_h=s(["Gabapentin","Carbamazepine (Tegretol)","Phenytoin","Pregabalin"],t.s)
B.a4W=new A.e("What is the first-line anti-convulsant for trigeminal neuralgia in MS?",B.a_h,1,"Carbamazepine (Tegretol) is the gold standard first-line treatment for trigeminal neuralgia, which presents as shooting, electric shock-type facial pain and is common in MS due to brainstem demyelination.")
B.Yg=s(["Lhermitte sign","Uhthoff phenomenon","Charcot triad","Marcus Gunn phenomenon"],t.s)
B.a6M=new A.e("What phenomenon causes temporary worsening of any MS symptom when body temperature rises?",B.Yg,1,"Uhthoff phenomenon occurs when elevated body temperature (from heat, exercise, or fever) slows neural conduction in demyelinated nerves, causing a temporary worsening of symptoms called a pseudo-exacerbation.")
B.R2=s(["Weak bladder with open sphincter; lesion in the sacral cord","Bladder contracts while sphincter simultaneously clamps shut; lesion between brainstem and sacral cord","Overactive bladder with weak sphincter; lesion in the frontal lobe","Flaccid bladder with no sphincter tone; lesion in the cauda equina"],t.s)
B.a9p=new A.e("In MS neurogenic bladder, what is detrusor sphincter dyssynergia (DSD) and where must the spinal lesion be located?",B.R2,1,"DSD is a coordination failure where the bladder contracts while the sphincter clamps shut simultaneously. The lesion must be between the sacral voiding center (S2-S4) and the pontine micturition center in the brainstem.")
B.V0=s([B.acX,B.abb,B.a4W,B.a6M,B.a9p],t.b)
B.Jx=new A.a8("Multiple Sclerosis 2",B.V0)
B.PJ=s(["C4","C5","T12","L1"],t.s)
B.abD=new A.e("What is the most common level of spinal cord injury overall?",B.PJ,1,"C5 is the most common level of spinal cord injury overall. T12 is the most common level specifically for paraplegia.")
B.Rm=s(["Dorsal columns","Lateral corticospinal tract","Lateral spinothalamic tract","Spinocerebellar tract"],t.s)
B.a7h=new A.e("Which tract carries pain and temperature sensation and crosses in the spinal cord within one to two segments of entering?",B.Rm,2,"The lateral spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature. It synapses in the dorsal horn and decussates within one to two segments via the anterior white commissure.")
B.YX=s(["Renal failure","Respiratory disorders (pneumonia)","Heart disease","Septicemia"],t.s)
B.a6J=new A.e("What is the current number one cause of death in the acute phase after spinal cord injury?",B.YX,1,"Respiratory disorders, specifically pneumonia, are now the number one killer in the acute phase. Renal failure used to be the leading cause before modern urological management.")
B.ZT=s(["Age at injury","Level of injury","Education level","Marital status"],t.s)
B.a48=new A.e("What is the single strongest predictor for return to work after spinal cord injury?",B.ZT,2,"Education level is the single strongest predictor for return to work after SCI, outweighing age and injury level.")
B.Su=s(["Medial to cervical fibers","Lateral to cervical fibers","Posterior to cervical fibers","Anterior to cervical fibers"],t.s)
B.a4P=new A.e("Within the lateral corticospinal tract, where are the sacral fibers located relative to the cervical fibers?",B.Su,1,"In the lateral corticospinal tract, sacral fibers are lateral (on the outside) and cervical fibers are medial (more central). This explains why central cord syndrome affects arms more than legs.")
B.OH=s([B.abD,B.a7h,B.a6J,B.a48,B.a4P],t.b)
B.Jy=new A.a8("Spinal Cord Injury 1",B.OH)
B.PA=s(["A factor of 2","A factor of 3","A factor of 4","A factor of 6"],t.s)
B.a3X=new A.e("Carpeting increases wheelchair rolling resistance by what factor compared to smooth surfaces?",B.PA,2,"Carpeting increases rolling resistance by a factor of 4 compared to smooth surfaces like tile or wood. Properly inflated pneumatic tires are the best choice for carpeted environments.")
B.WY=s(["0 degrees (perfectly vertical)","2 to 5 degrees","10 to 15 degrees","20 degrees or more"],t.s)
B.a6U=new A.e("The typical camber prescription for everyday wheelchair use is:",B.WY,1,"For everyday use, 2-5 degrees of camber provides stability, ergonomic push position, and hand protection without making the chair too wide. Higher angles (10-15 degrees) are reserved for sports.")
B.X0=s(["A joystick mounted on one armrest","Two handrims on the same wheel - outer rim drives that wheel, inner rim drives the opposite wheel, both together goes straight","A lever system like rowing","Foot steering with one-handed propulsion"],t.s)
B.a6S=new A.e("In a one-arm drive wheelchair, how does the patient steer and propel?",B.X0,1,"Two handrims are mounted on one wheel. The outer rim propels that wheel, the inner rim propels the opposite wheel via a special axle, and grabbing both engages a clutch to go straight.")
B.ZC=s(["They wear out faster","They get caught in small cracks and gaps in sidewalks, causing abrupt stops","They are too expensive","They add too much weight"],t.s)
B.aak=new A.e("Why are smaller casters (2-4 inches) problematic despite their maneuverability advantage?",B.ZC,1,"Small casters are extremely maneuverable but get stuck in sidewalk cracks and gaps. Larger casters (5-8 inches) roll over obstacles more easily but reduce turning ability.")
B.Y8=s(["Add extra weight to the footrests","Move the rear axle posteriorly to shift the center of gravity forward","Use smaller rear wheels","Lower the seat height"],t.s)
B.abz=new A.e("For a bilateral lower extremity amputee, what wheelchair modification prevents backward tipping?",B.Y8,1,"Without legs, the center of gravity shifts posteriorly. Moving the rear axle back (or adding anti-tip casters) compensates by shifting the base of support behind the new center of gravity, preventing backward tipping.")
B.ZI=s([B.a3X,B.a6U,B.a6S,B.aak,B.abz],t.b)
B.Jz=new A.a8("Wheelchair 2",B.ZI)
B.RE=s(["Bilateral sternocleidomastoid muscles","Right sternocleidomastoid and left splenius capitis","Right (ipsilateral) splenius capitis and left (contralateral) sternocleidomastoid","Bilateral splenius capitis muscles"],t.s)
B.abZ=new A.e("In cervical dystonia with rotational torticollis to the right, which two muscles are the primary targets for botulinum toxin injection?",B.RE,2,"For right rotational torticollis, the ipsilateral (right) splenius capitis rotates the head to the same side, while the contralateral (left) sternocleidomastoid is a contralateral rotator. Together they produce the rightward chin rotation.")
B.XY=s(["Older patients always have worse outcomes","The younger the onset, the higher the likelihood of progression from focal to generalized dystonia","Children never develop generalized dystonia","Age of onset does not affect prognosis"],t.s)
B.a5t=new A.e('What is the defining prognostic principle regarding age of onset in dystonia (the "age rule")?',B.XY,1,"The age rule states that younger age of onset correlates with higher likelihood of progression from focal to generalized dystonia. A child with foot dystonia is at high risk for generalized disease, while a 50-year-old with focal neck dystonia tends to stay focal.")
B.Sh=s(["Ferritin directly measures dopamine levels in the brain","Iron is a required cofactor for tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme in dopamine synthesis","Low ferritin causes peripheral neuropathy that mimics RLS","Ferritin is needed to rule out hemochromatosis as a cause of RLS"],t.s)
B.a6Q=new A.e("In restless leg syndrome, why is checking a serum ferritin level essential even when the CBC shows a normal hemoglobin?",B.Sh,1,"Tyrosine hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme for dopamine production and requires iron as a cofactor. A patient can have a normal hemoglobin but still have insufficient brain iron for dopamine synthesis. Ferritin should be checked and treated if below 50-75.")
B.Qx=s(["A dystonia that worsens with levodopa, requiring immediate surgical intervention","A genetic subset of dystonia that responds dramatically to low-dose levodopa, essentially providing a cure","A form of Parkinson's disease that presents with dystonic features in elderly patients","A medication side effect from chronic dopamine agonist use"],t.s)
B.a89=new A.e("What is dopa-responsive dystonia (DRD), and why is it critical to identify?",B.Qx,1,"Dopa-responsive dystonia is a specific genetic form that often presents in childhood with a dystonic gait that may worsen in the evening. It responds remarkably well to very low doses of levodopa. Missing this diagnosis denies the patient an effective treatment.")
B.YD=s(["Serotonin excess and norepinephrine deficiency","GABA excess and glutamate deficiency","Dopamine deficiency and relative acetylcholine (cholinergic) excess","Acetylcholine deficiency and dopamine excess"],t.s)
B.a6l=new A.e("In Parkinson's disease, the motor symptoms result from an imbalance between which two neurotransmitter systems?",B.YD,2,"Parkinson's involves progressive loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra. Dopamine normally inhibits the cholinergic system. When dopamine drops, the seesaw tips toward relative cholinergic excess, driving tremor and rigidity.")
B.a_v=s([B.abZ,B.a5t,B.a6Q,B.a89,B.a6l],t.b)
B.JA=new A.a8("Spasticity 3",B.a_v)
B.TR=s(["FDS and FDP","EPL and EPB","APL and EPB (first dorsal compartment)","ECRL and ECRB"],t.s)
B.ab2=new A.e("De Quervain's tenosynovitis involves which tendons?",B.TR,2,'De Quervain\'s involves the first dorsal compartment: abductor pollicis longus (APL) and extensor pollicis brevis (EPB). The Finkelstein test (ulnar deviation with thumb in fist) reproduces pain. Common in new mothers ("mommy thumb").')
B.Z6=s(["Tendon rupture","Thickening of the A1 pulley with tendon nodule entrapment","Dupuytren's contracture","Joint dislocation"],t.s)
B.a9d=new A.e("Trigger finger is caused by:",B.Z6,1,"Trigger finger results from thickening of the A1 pulley and/or nodule formation on the flexor tendon. The tendon catches as it passes through the narrowed pulley, causing clicking, locking, or a fixed flexion contracture.")
B.Ph=s(["The flexor tendons","The palmar fascia (palmar aponeurosis)","The extensor tendons","The joint capsule"],t.s)
B.a92=new A.e("Dupuytren's contracture primarily involves:",B.Ph,1,"Dupuytren's is a fibroproliferative disorder of the palmar fascia causing nodules and cords that progressively flex the MCP and PIP joints. The ring finger is most commonly affected. Risk factors include Northern European descent, diabetes, alcohol use, and smoking.")
B.ZL=s(["Radial collateral ligament of the thumb MCP","Ulnar collateral ligament of the thumb MCP","Volar plate of the thumb IP joint","Scapholunate ligament"],t.s)
B.a6Y=new A.e("A Gamekeeper's (Skier's) thumb involves injury to which structure?",B.ZL,1,"Gamekeeper's thumb is a sprain/tear of the UCL of the thumb MCP joint. Caused by forceful abduction (ski pole falls, breaking a fall). A Stener lesion (adductor aponeurosis interposition) requires surgical repair as it prevents ligament healing.")
B.N8=s(["It is the largest carpal bone","Its retrograde blood supply puts it at high risk for avascular necrosis","It articulates with no other carpal bones","It is always visible on initial X-ray"],t.s)
B.a7_=new A.e("The scaphoid fracture is significant because:",B.N8,1,"The scaphoid has a retrograde blood supply (entering distally via the dorsal ridge). Proximal pole fractures are at highest risk for AVN. Scaphoid fractures may not be visible on initial X-rays \u2014 suspect with anatomical snuffbox tenderness and repeat imaging at 2 weeks or get MRI.")
B.Zu=s([B.ab2,B.a9d,B.a92,B.a6Y,B.a7_],t.b)
B.JB=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Hand",B.Zu)
B.Qr=s(["It weighs more, providing inertia","It achieves foot flat faster, moving the GRF anterior to the knee","It has a rigid keel","It provides energy return during toe-off"],t.s)
B.a8n=new A.e("Why does a single-axis prosthetic foot improve knee stability for a transfemoral amputee?",B.Qr,1,"The single-axis foot plantar flexes rapidly at heel strike, getting foot flat faster and moving the ground reaction force anterior to the knee axis for stability.")
B.Td=s(["Too heavy for most patients","Allows the femur to abduct within the socket, reducing hip abductor effectiveness","Does not provide adequate suspension","Compresses the sciatic nerve"],t.s)
B.a9j=new A.e("What is the fundamental flaw of the quadrilateral socket design?",B.Td,1,"The wide mediolateral dimension allows the femur to drift outward, putting hip abductors on slack and causing Trendelenburg gait.")
B.YN=s(["Suction suspension","TES belt","Silesian belt","Pelvic band with hip joint"],t.s)
B.a4x=new A.e("Which suspension system is contraindicated in a patient with a femoral bypass graft?",B.YN,3,"A pelvic band with hip joint should not be used on a femoral bypass graft patient due to pressure risk.")
B.Zc=s(["Too heavy","Cannot unweight both legs simultaneously to sit","Not enough swing phase control","Requires too much cable excursion"],t.s)
B.acR=new A.e("Why can a stance control knee NOT be prescribed for a bilateral transfemoral amputee?",B.Zc,1,"Stance control knees use weight-activated brakes. With bilateral knees, you cannot unweight both legs to disengage both brakes for sitting.")
B.Th=s(["Lighter weight","Holds the femur in slight adduction, optimizing hip abductor length","More hip ROM","Better end bearing"],t.s)
B.a7o=new A.e("What biomechanical advantage does the ischial containment socket provide over the quadrilateral socket?",B.Th,1,"The ICS holds the femur in slight adduction, pre-stretching the hip abductors to optimal length-tension for pelvis stabilization.")
B.TG=s([B.a8n,B.a9j,B.a4x,B.acR,B.a7o],t.b)
B.JC=new A.a8("Prosthetics & Orthotics 5",B.TG)
B.Vb=s(["Anterior cerebral artery (ACA)","Middle cerebral artery (MCA)","Posterior cerebral artery (PCA)","Basilar artery"],t.s)
B.a8w=new A.e("Which artery supplies the lateral surface of the cerebral hemisphere and is the most commonly affected vessel in ischemic stroke?",B.Vb,1,"The MCA is the most commonly affected artery in ischemic stroke. It supplies the lateral hemisphere including the motor and sensory cortex for face and upper extremity, Broca's area, and Wernicke's area.")
B.XM=s(["Middle cerebral artery","Anterior cerebral artery","Posterior cerebral artery","Posterior inferior cerebellar artery"],t.s)
B.a81=new A.e("A patient presents with contralateral leg weakness and sensory loss with relative sparing of the face and arm. Which vascular territory is most likely affected?",B.XM,1,"The ACA supplies the medial surface of the hemisphere, which includes the motor and sensory representation of the lower extremity. ACA strokes classically present with contralateral leg weakness greater than arm or face.")
B.TE=s(["Anterior cerebral artery","Middle cerebral artery","Posterior cerebral artery","Anterior choroidal artery"],t.s)
B.acM=new A.e("Homonymous hemianopia without motor deficits is most characteristic of a stroke in which vascular territory?",B.TE,2,"The PCA supplies the occipital lobe (visual cortex). PCA strokes classically present with contralateral homonymous hemianopia. Motor deficits are typically absent since the motor cortex is supplied by the ACA and MCA.")
B.PR=s(["Anterior communicating arteries","Posterior communicating arteries","Ophthalmic arteries","Lenticulostriate arteries"],t.s)
B.aay=new A.e("In the Circle of Willis, which arteries connect the anterior and posterior circulation?",B.PR,1,"The posterior communicating arteries (PComm) connect the internal carotid arteries (anterior circulation) to the posterior cerebral arteries (posterior/vertebrobasilar circulation). The anterior communicating artery connects the two ACAs.")
B.Up=s(["Large vessel atherosclerotic stroke","Lacunar infarct","Cardioembolic stroke","Watershed infarct"],t.s)
B.a9G=new A.e("Which type of ischemic stroke is caused by cardiogenic embolism and is associated with atrial fibrillation?",B.Up,2,"Cardioembolic strokes account for approximately 20% of ischemic strokes and are strongly associated with atrial fibrillation. The embolus typically lodges in the MCA territory. Treatment includes anticoagulation rather than antiplatelet therapy alone.")
B.OZ=s([B.a8w,B.a81,B.acM,B.aay,B.a9G],t.b)
B.JD=new A.a8("Cerebrovascular Accident 2",B.OZ)
B.Vp=s(["Hereditary shows conduction block; acquired shows uniform slowing","Hereditary shows uniform slowing; acquired shows conduction block and temporal dispersion","There is no electrodiagnostic difference","Hereditary affects sensory nerves only"],t.s)
B.a9S=new A.e("The key NCS distinction between hereditary and acquired demyelinating neuropathies is:",B.Vp,1,"Hereditary demyelinating neuropathies (CMT1) show uniform slowing across all nerves without conduction block or temporal dispersion. Acquired demyelinating neuropathies (GBS, CIDP) show patchy, non-uniform slowing WITH conduction block and temporal dispersion.")
B.VQ=s(["SOD1","Dystrophin","PMP-22","SMN1"],t.s)
B.a7q=new A.e("Charcot-Marie-Tooth Type 1 is caused by duplication of which gene on chromosome 17?",B.VQ,2,"CMT1 (the most common hereditary neuropathy) is caused by PMP-22 gene duplication on chromosome 17. This produces too much peripheral myelin protein, leading to abnormal myelination. Conduction velocities are severely and uniformly slowed (<38 m/s).")
B.Q5=s(["PMP-22 duplication","PMP-22 deletion","SOD1 mutation","SMN1 deletion"],t.s)
B.a6q=new A.e("Hereditary Neuropathy with Liability to Pressure Palsies (HNPP) is caused by:",B.Q5,1,"HNPP is caused by PMP-22 deletion \u2014 the reciprocal of CMT1 (which is PMP-22 duplication). HNPP patients develop prolonged nerve palsies (weeks-long foot drop or wrist drop) from minor compression that would not affect normal individuals.")
B.MW=s(["Low protein, high white cells","High protein, normal cell count (albuminocytologic dissociation)","Normal protein, high white cells","Low protein, low glucose"],t.s)
B.a9k=new A.e("Guillain-Barr\xe9 syndrome (GBS) is characterized by which CSF finding?",B.MW,1,"GBS shows albuminocytologic dissociation: elevated CSF protein with normal cell count. This classic CSF pattern, combined with ascending weakness and areflexia, is highly suggestive of GBS. Treatment is IVIG or plasmapheresis \u2014 NOT steroids.")
B.Un=s(["EMG findings","CSF protein levels","Time course \u2014 GBS peaks by 4 weeks; CIDP continues beyond 8 weeks","Presence of conduction block"],t.s)
B.a4i=new A.e("The distinction between GBS and CIDP is primarily based on:",B.Un,2,"GBS is acute \u2014 it reaches nadir within 4 weeks. CIDP (Chronic Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyneuropathy) progresses beyond 8 weeks and has a relapsing-remitting or chronic progressive course. Unlike GBS, CIDP responds to steroids.")
B.S3=s([B.a9S,B.a7q,B.a6q,B.a9k,B.a4i],t.b)
B.JE=new A.a8("Electrodiagnostic 12",B.S3)
B.OW=s(["0 to 5 PSI","5 to 25 PSI","25 to 50 PSI","50 to 75 PSI"],t.s)
B.a8i=new A.e("During provocation discography, what is the normal disc opening pressure range?",B.OW,1,"Normal opening pressure should be between 5 to 25 PSI. Pressures above 30 PSI suggest the needle tip is still in the annulus.")
B.Qw=s(["Less than 15 PSI","15 to 50 PSI","51 to 90 PSI","Greater than 90 PSI"],t.s)
B.a6x=new A.e('In discography, a "chemical disc" produces concordant pain at what pressure threshold above opening pressure?',B.Qw,0,"A chemical disc produces concordant pain at very low pressures, less than 15 PSI above opening pressure, indicating extreme sensitization.")
B.No=s(["C3 to T1","T1 to L2","T6 to S2","L1 to S4"],t.s)
B.a64=new A.e("From which spinal levels do presynaptic sympathetic fibers originate?",B.No,1,"Presynaptic sympathetic fibers arise from the intermediolateral cell column at T1 to L2 only.")
B.Tq=s(["Concordant is more severe","Concordant reproduces the patient's usual clinical symptoms","Non-concordant occurs at lower pressures","Non-concordant always indicates pathology"],t.s)
B.a5Z=new A.e("What distinguishes concordant from non-concordant pain during discography?",B.Tq,1,"Concordant pain reproduces the patient's exact usual symptoms; non-concordant is just procedural pain.")
B.YJ=s(["They carry parasympathetic fibers","They contain myelinated nerve fibers","Located near white matter","They carry sensory information"],t.s)
B.acv=new A.e('Why are the white rami communicantes called "white"?',B.YJ,1,"White rami contain myelinated nerve fibers with fatty myelin insulation that makes signals travel fast.")
B.Y1=s([B.a8i,B.a6x,B.a64,B.a5Z,B.acv],t.b)
B.JF=new A.a8("Pain 4",B.Y1)
B.UB=s(["Iliopsoas tendon inflammation","Gluteus medius/minimus tendinopathy","Hamstring tendon avulsion","IT band snapping"],t.s)
B.a8B=new A.e("Greater trochanteric pain syndrome (formerly trochanteric bursitis) most commonly involves:",B.UB,1,'Greater trochanteric pain syndrome is now understood to primarily involve gluteus medius and minimus tendinopathy rather than isolated bursitis. The tendons insert on the greater trochanter and can undergo degenerative changes similar to rotator cuff disease ("rotator cuff of the hip").')
B.Tm=s(["Quadriceps tendon and patella","Hip joint and sacroiliac joint","Lumbar spine and sciatic nerve","Knee meniscus and ACL"],t.s)
B.a5W=new A.e("The FABER (Patrick) test assesses for pathology in which structures?",B.Tm,1,"FABER = Flexion, ABduction, External Rotation. Pain in the groin = hip joint pathology. Pain in the posterior buttock = sacroiliac joint pathology. This test stresses both structures simultaneously.")
B.Wj=s(["Excessive acetabular coverage (overcoverage)","Abnormal bony prominence on the femoral head-neck junction","Labral hypertrophy","Femoral head AVN"],t.s)
B.a3W=new A.e("Femoroacetabular impingement (FAI) of the cam type involves:",B.Wj,1,"Cam impingement: abnormal bone on the femoral head-neck junction (loss of normal concavity) that jams into the acetabulum during flexion. Pincer impingement: excessive acetabular coverage. Many patients have mixed (cam + pincer) type.")
B.Ou=s(["Originates from the piriformis muscle","Passes through or under the piriformis muscle","Is compressed by the piriformis at the knee","Is a branch of the piriformis nerve"],t.s)
B.a9Z=new A.e("Piriformis syndrome can mimic sciatica because the sciatic nerve:",B.Ou,1,"The sciatic nerve typically passes beneath the piriformis muscle. In anatomic variants (~15-20% of population), the nerve (or its peroneal division) may pass through or above the piriformis. Spasm or hypertrophy of the piriformis can compress the nerve, causing buttock pain radiating down the leg.")
B.Rx=s(["They always heal with rest alone","They are at higher risk of displacement and require surgical fixation","They are less painful than compression-side fractures","They only occur in elderly patients"],t.s)
B.act=new A.e("Stress fractures of the femoral neck on the tension side (superior/lateral) are important because:",B.Rx,1,"Tension-side (superior) femoral neck stress fractures are at high risk for displacement and completion \u2014 they require prophylactic surgical fixation. Compression-side (inferior/medial) fractures are lower risk and can often be managed conservatively with protected weight-bearing.")
B.VO=s([B.a8B,B.a5W,B.a3W,B.a9Z,B.act],t.b)
B.JG=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Hip 2",B.VO)
B.Vc=s(["1 centimeter","2-3 centimeters","4-5 centimeters","At the same level"],t.s)
B.a5Y=new A.e("In a transtibial amputation, the fibula should be cut how much shorter than the tibia?",B.Vc,1,"The fibula must be cut 2-3 centimeters shorter than the tibia because it is not weight-bearing.")
B.Wq=s(["Less than 15 seconds","Greater than 20 seconds","Greater than 25 seconds","Greater than 30 seconds"],t.s)
B.abS=new A.e("What venous filling time threshold suggests inadequate arterial inflow for wound healing?",B.Wq,2,"Venous filling time should be less than 25 seconds; longer indicates too slow arterial inflow for healing.")
B.Np=s(["Calcaneovalgus","Equinovarus","Pes planus","Hallux valgus"],t.s)
B.ad9=new A.e("A Chopart amputation commonly results in what deformity due to unopposed calf muscle pull?",B.Np,1,"Unopposed gastrocnemius/soleus pull creates equinovarus deformity after Chopart amputation.")
B.Zx=s(["Diabetes mellitus","Knee flexion contracture greater than 50 degrees","Age over 70","Absence of palpable pedal pulses"],t.s)
B.aaS=new A.e("What is a contraindication for performing a BKA in a vascular patient?",B.Zx,1,"A knee flexion contracture >50 degrees with ischemia contraindicates BKA \u2014 the knee will never straighten enough for prosthetic use.")
B.TB=s(["Better cosmetic appearance","End bearing capability allowing ambulation without a prosthesis","Less surgical time","Lower risk of phantom pain"],t.s)
B.a5s=new A.e("What is the key advantage of a Symes amputation compared to a transtibial amputation?",B.TB,1,"Symes preserves the heel pad on the tibia, allowing end bearing and short-distance ambulation without a prosthesis.")
B.R9=s([B.a5Y,B.abS,B.ad9,B.aaS,B.a5s],t.b)
B.JH=new A.a8("Prosthetics & Orthotics 4",B.R9)
B.Sv=s(["Upper motor neuron signs only","Lower motor neuron signs only","Both upper and lower motor neuron signs in the same patient","Sensory and motor deficits"],t.s)
B.a5L=new A.e("ALS is characterized by the combination of:",B.Sv,2,"ALS uniquely combines UMN signs (spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski) AND LMN signs (atrophy, fasciculations, fibrillations) in the same patient, often in the same limb. This combination is the hallmark that distinguishes ALS from pure UMN or LMN diseases.")
B.YI=s(["Motor, sensory, and autonomic","Sensation, bowel/bladder, and eye movements","Cognition, respiration, and swallowing","Reflexes, coordination, and balance"],t.s)
B.a9w=new A.e("Which three systems are characteristically SPARED in ALS?",B.YI,1,"The ALS sparing triad: (1) Sensation \u2014 SNAPs are normal, (2) Bowel/bladder \u2014 sphincters spared until very late, (3) Eye movements \u2014 extraocular muscles spared until end-stage. If any of these are prominently affected early, reconsider the diagnosis.")
B.TT=s(["1 of 4 regions","2 of 4 regions","3 of 4 regions","All 4 regions"],t.s)
B.a8Q=new A.e("To diagnose ALS using the El Escorial criteria, EMG must show active denervation and chronic reinnervation in at least how many body regions?",B.TT,2,"El Escorial criteria require findings in 3 of 4 body segments: (1) bulbar/cranial, (2) cervical, (3) thoracic, (4) lumbosacral. Each region must show both active denervation (fibrillations/PSWs) and chronic reinnervation (large polyphasic MUAPs).")
B.RV=s(["Always fatal","Treatable with IVIG and shows conduction block on NCS","Identical to ALS on EMG","Associated with sensory loss"],t.s)
B.a46=new A.e("Multifocal motor neuropathy (MMN) is an important ALS mimic because it is:",B.RV,1,"MMN is treatable with IVIG \u2014 making it critical to distinguish from ALS. Key features: conduction block on NCS (ALS has NO conduction block), anti-GM1 antibodies, and pure motor involvement. Unlike ALS, MMN has a normal life expectancy with treatment.")
B.Nn=s(["Birth to 6 months","6-18 months","After 18 months","Adolescence"],t.s)
B.aaH=new A.e("Spinal Muscular Atrophy Type I (Werdnig-Hoffmann) presents at what age?",B.Nn,0,"SMA Type I (Werdnig-Hoffmann): onset before 6 months, floppy baby, never achieves sitting, most severe. Type II: 6-18 months, can sit but never walks. Type III (Kugelberg-Welander): after 18 months, walks then loses function. All are autosomal recessive (SMN1 gene).")
B.Zl=s([B.a5L,B.a9w,B.a8Q,B.a46,B.aaH],t.b)
B.JI=new A.a8("Electrodiagnostic 15",B.Zl)
B.Rg=s(["Plain X-ray","Serum alkaline phosphatase level","Triple phase bone scan","MRI of the affected joint"],t.s)
B.a5S=new A.e("What is the most sensitive test for early diagnosis of heterotopic ossification (HO)?",B.Rg,2,"The triple phase bone scan is the most sensitive test for early HO diagnosis. It detects increased blood flow and bone formation weeks before plain X-rays show anything. X-rays lag by 7-10 days after clinical signs appear.")
B.a_6=s(["Apply SCDs immediately to prevent further clot formation","Perform a venous duplex ultrasound before applying SCDs","Start low molecular weight heparin and skip mechanical prophylaxis","Place an IVC filter prophylactically"],t.s)
B.a97=new A.e("A patient with acute SCI was not placed on sequential compression devices (SCDs) for 72 hours after admission. What is the appropriate next step?",B.a_6,1,"If SCDs were not applied within 72 hours, a DVT may have already formed. Applying compression could dislodge the clot and cause a pulmonary embolism. A venous duplex ultrasound must be performed to rule out DVT before starting mechanical compression.")
B.Q6=s(["Until hospital discharge","4 weeks","8 weeks","12 weeks"],t.s)
B.a7u=new A.e("What is the recommended duration of DVT chemical prophylaxis for a patient with a complete spinal cord injury and no additional risk factors?",B.Q6,2,"For complete but uncomplicated SCI, the recommendation is 8 weeks of chemical prophylaxis. Complicated complete injuries (with fractures, cancer, obesity, heart failure, or age >70) should receive 12 weeks. Incomplete ambulatory patients can stop at discharge.")
B.PG=s(["As soon as the joint is functionally limited","When the alkaline phosphatase peaks at 10 weeks","When the bone scan normalizes and ALP returns to normal, typically 12-18 months","Within the first 6 weeks to prevent ankylosis"],t.s)
B.acF=new A.e("When is it safe to surgically excise heterotopic ossification (HO)?",B.PG,2,"Surgery must wait until the HO has matured, typically 12-18 months. A normal (cold) bone scan and normalized ALP confirm maturity. Operating on immature HO leads to high recurrence rates because the active bone formation process will restart.")
B.QI=s(["Gabapentin","Amitriptyline","Pregabalin (Lyrica)","Tramadol"],t.s)
B.abw=new A.e("According to the Canadian pain guidelines discussed, what is the only FDA-approved medication specifically for SCI neuropathic pain?",B.QI,2,"Pregabalin (Lyrica) is the only FDA-approved drug specifically for SCI neuropathic pain. Gabapentin and amitriptyline are also first-line agents but do not carry this specific FDA indication.")
B.ZE=s([B.a5S,B.a97,B.a7u,B.acF,B.abw],t.b)
B.JJ=new A.a8("Spinal Cord Injury 9",B.ZE)
B.RX=s(["The neuromuscular junction","The brainstem","The spinal cord anterior horn cells","The muscle fibers directly"],t.s)
B.aal=new A.e("What is the primary site of action of cyclobenzaprine (Flexeril) that distinguishes it from baclofen and tizanidine?",B.RX,1,"Cyclobenzaprine acts primarily at the level of the brainstem, reducing tonic somatic motor activity. This distinguishes it from baclofen and tizanidine, which act primarily at the spinal cord level.")
B.Wp=s(["Less than 1%","Greater than 3%","Greater than 5%","Greater than 10%"],t.s)
B.abl=new A.e("At what concentration does phenol transition from acting as a local anesthetic to causing true neurolysis with Wallerian degeneration?",B.Wp,2,"At concentrations less than 3%, phenol acts only as a local anesthetic with transient effects. At concentrations greater than 5%, phenol causes protein denaturation and tissue necrosis, leading to Wallerian degeneration and effects lasting six months or longer.")
B.PS=s(["They have no CNS side effects","They can improve spasticity without causing a significant decrease in muscle power","They work faster than all other oral medications","They are the only FDA-approved agents for spasticity"],t.s)
B.a6d=new A.e("What is the unique advantage of cannabinoids (dronabinol) for spasticity management compared to other oral agents like dantrolene?",B.PS,1,"Cannabinoids can reduce spasticity without significantly decreasing muscle power, which is important for patients who need every bit of strength for functional tasks like transfers. Dantrolene actively weakens muscle, and high-dose baclofen can cause generalized weakness.")
B.Sy=s(["To test the patient's pain tolerance for the procedure","To determine if the problem is neural spasticity versus fixed contracture, and whether the spasticity is functionally useful","To identify the correct nerve using electrical stimulation","To reduce the dose of phenol needed for the permanent block"],t.s)
B.a8G=new A.e("Before performing a permanent chemoneurolysis procedure, why is a diagnostic nerve block with lidocaine recommended first?",B.Sy,1,"A diagnostic block with lidocaine serves two purposes: (1) if the nerve is blocked and the joint still does not move, the problem is a fixed contracture, not spasticity; (2) it reveals whether the spasticity was functionally useful -- if the patient's transfers worsen after the block, the spasticity should be preserved.")
B.Xf=s(["It potentiates GABA-A receptor activity","It does not bind to GABA receptors at all","It irreversibly inhibits GABA transaminase","It acts as a direct GABA-B agonist like baclofen"],t.s)
B.acg=new A.e("Despite being a structural derivative of GABA, gabapentin has which surprising pharmacologic property?",B.Xf,1,"Despite being a GABA analog, gabapentin does not bind to GABA receptors. Instead, it binds to the alpha-2 delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, likely inhibiting neurotransmitter release including glutamate.")
B.Qm=s([B.aal,B.abl,B.a6d,B.a8G,B.acg],t.b)
B.JK=new A.a8("Spasticity 2",B.Qm)
B.Sm=s(["6 of 18","9 of 18","11 of 18","14 of 18"],t.s)
B.abg=new A.e("Under the 1990 ACR criteria for fibromyalgia, how many of the 18 tender points must be positive?",B.Sm,2,"The 1990 ACR criteria required tenderness in at least 11 of 18 specific anatomical tender points plus widespread pain in all four body quadrants and axial involvement.")
B.Yu=s(["Warmth, erythema, and swelling","Palpable taut band, referred pain pattern, and local twitch response","Numbness, weakness, and atrophy","Joint crepitus, locking, and instability"],t.s)
B.a8P=new A.e("What are the three pathognomonic features of a trigger point that distinguish it from a tender point in fibromyalgia?",B.Yu,1,"A trigger point has: (1) a palpable taut band of muscle, (2) a referred pain pattern (pain travels to a distant area), and (3) a local twitch response when the band is snapped. Tender points in fibromyalgia only hurt where pressed.")
B.TS=s(["Added more tender point locations","Replaced tender point exam with Widespread Pain Index (WPI) and Symptom Severity Score (SSS) including fatigue, cognitive symptoms, and unrefreshed sleep","Required positive lab markers","Added MRI criteria"],t.s)
B.a5q=new A.e("How did the 2010 ACR fibromyalgia criteria change from the 1990 version?",B.TS,1,"The 2010 criteria moved away from the 11/18 tender point exam to the WPI (19 body areas with pain) and SSS (fatigue, waking unrefreshed, cognitive symptoms). Symptoms must be present for at least 3 months.")
B.R3=s(["Fibromyalgia is always bilateral; myofascial is always unilateral","Myofascial pain is localized to a specific muscle group; fibromyalgia is diffuse and widespread","Fibromyalgia has elevated CRP; myofascial does not","Myofascial pain always involves joints; fibromyalgia does not"],t.s)
B.adf=new A.e("What is the key clinical difference between fibromyalgia and myofascial pain syndrome?",B.R3,1,"Myofascial pain syndrome involves localized pain with trigger points in a specific muscle group (e.g., just the right trapezius). Fibromyalgia is diffuse and widespread. Myofascial pain often resolves with local treatment; fibromyalgia requires systemic management.")
B.UW=s(["WPI >= 7 and SSS >= 5, OR WPI 3-6 and SSS >= 9","WPI >= 10 and SSS >= 3","WPI >= 5 and SSS >= 7","WPI >= 12 and SSS >= 2"],t.s)
B.a5r=new A.e("For fibromyalgia symptoms present at least 3 months, what WPI and SSS score combinations can satisfy the 2010 diagnostic criteria?",B.UW,0,"The 2010 criteria require either WPI >= 7 with SSS >= 5, or WPI 3-6 with SSS >= 9. This allows patients with fewer pain areas but severe fatigue and cognitive symptoms to still be diagnosed.")
B.VR=s([B.abg,B.a8P,B.a5q,B.adf,B.a5r],t.b)
B.JL=new A.a8("Rheumatology 6",B.VR)
B.NI=s(["Shoulder abduction and elbow flexion","Wrist extension and biceps (elbow flexion)","Triceps and wrist flexion","Hand intrinsic muscles"],t.s)
B.a71=new A.e("A C6 radiculopathy presents with weakness of:",B.NI,1,"C6 radiculopathy: weakness of wrist extension (ECRL/ECRB) and biceps (elbow flexion). Sensory loss is in the thumb and lateral forearm. The biceps and brachioradialis reflexes are diminished. The most common cause is C5-C6 disc herniation.")
B.Q4=s(["Cervical myelopathy","Non-organic (psychogenic) components of low back pain","Cauda equina syndrome","Lumbar spinal stenosis"],t.s)
B.adg=new A.e("The Waddell signs are used to identify:",B.Q4,1,"Waddell signs (5 categories): superficial/non-anatomic tenderness, simulation tests (axial loading, rotation), distraction tests (SLR discrepancy), regional disturbances (non-dermatomal weakness/sensory loss), and overreaction. Three or more positive categories suggest non-organic factors.")
B.OD=s(["Cervical spine pain","Lumbar disc herniation","Sacroiliac joint inflammation (sacroiliitis)","Peripheral joint arthritis"],t.s)
B.a7U=new A.e("Ankylosing spondylitis most commonly begins with:",B.OD,2,"Ankylosing spondylitis (HLA-B27 associated) typically begins with bilateral sacroiliitis in young adults (20-30 years). It progresses cranially with bamboo spine appearance. Morning stiffness >30 minutes that improves with exercise is characteristic. It affects males more than females.")
B.On=s(["Dens (odontoid) fracture","Burst fracture of the C1 (atlas) ring","Hangman's fracture of C2","Compression fracture of C7"],t.s)
B.a4H=new A.e("The Jefferson fracture involves:",B.On,1,"The Jefferson fracture is a burst fracture of the C1 (atlas) ring from axial loading (diving into shallow water, hitting head on car roof). It typically involves fractures of both anterior and posterior arches. It is usually stable because the ring fragments spread outward.")
B.Vt=s(["C1 (atlas)","C2 (axis) \u2014 bilateral pars interarticularis fractures","C3","C7"],t.s)
B.a7H=new A.e("A hangman's fracture involves which vertebra?",B.Vt,1,"A hangman's fracture is a bilateral fracture of the C2 pars interarticularis (pedicles) from hyperextension and axial loading. Despite the name, it is now most commonly caused by motor vehicle accidents. It is often neurologically intact because the fracture widens the canal.")
