B.YS=s(["Supraspinatus","Infraspinatus","Deltoid","Teres minor"],t.s)
B.aaG=new A.e("The rotator cuff is composed of four muscles. Which muscle is NOT part of the rotator cuff?",B.YS,2,"The rotator cuff consists of the SITS muscles: Supraspinatus, Infraspinatus, Teres minor, and Subscapularis. The deltoid is the primary shoulder abductor but is not part of the rotator cuff.")
B.Nk=s(["Subscapularis","Infraspinatus","Supraspinatus","Teres minor"],t.s)
B.a8l=new A.e("Which rotator cuff muscle is most commonly torn?",B.Nk,2,'The supraspinatus is most commonly torn due to its location in the subacromial space and its relatively poor blood supply in the "critical zone" near its insertion on the greater tuberosity.')
B.Yn=s(["Infraspinatus","Subscapularis","Supraspinatus","Teres minor"],t.s)
B.abT=new A.e('The "empty can" test primarily evaluates which muscle?',B.Yn,2,"The empty can (Jobe) test: arm at 90\xb0 abduction, 30\xb0 forward flexion, internally rotated (thumb down). Resist downward pressure. Weakness or pain = supraspinatus pathology.")
B.Qj=s(["Reduced blood supply","Inherent instability","Limited nerve innervation","Decreased muscle attachment points"],t.s)
B.a5C=new A.e("The glenohumeral joint has the greatest range of motion of any joint in the body. What is the primary trade-off?",B.Qj,1,"The glenohumeral joint sacrifices stability for mobility. The glenoid fossa is shallow (covers only ~25% of the humeral head), making it the most commonly dislocated joint. Stability relies on the labrum, capsule, rotator cuff, and ligaments.")
B.Qt=s(["Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"],t.s)
B.a3Z=new A.e("Anterior shoulder dislocations most commonly injure which nerve?",B.Qt,2,'The axillary nerve wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus and is most vulnerable during anterior dislocations. Test for deltoid weakness and decreased sensation over the lateral "regimental badge" area. Incidence increases with age.')
