B.S8=s(["Bone marrow only","Skin, muscle, and nerve in the radiation field","The entire body systemically","Only lymphatic tissue"],t.s)
B.aaM=new A.e("Radiation fibrosis syndrome most commonly affects which tissues?",B.S8,1,"Radiation fibrosis is a progressive process affecting skin, subcutaneous tissue, muscle, and nerves within the radiation field. It can cause contractures, neuropathy, and lymphedema. It may present months to years after radiation therapy.")
B.Yd=s(["Diuretic therapy","Surgical debulking","Complete decongestive therapy (CDT)","Compression stockings alone"],t.s)
B.aaW=new A.e("The cornerstone treatment for cancer-related lymphedema is:",B.Yd,2,"Complete decongestive therapy (CDT) is the gold standard for lymphedema management. It includes manual lymphatic drainage, compression bandaging, exercise, and skin care. Diuretics are generally not effective for lymphedema.")
B.SS=s(["Gentle exercise","Blood pressure measurement and venipuncture","Wearing loose clothing","Elevation of the extremity"],t.s)
B.a9y=new A.e("A patient with a history of breast cancer and axillary node dissection should avoid which of the following in the affected arm?",B.SS,1,"Blood pressure measurement, venipuncture, and IV access should be avoided in the affected arm after axillary lymph node dissection to reduce lymphedema risk. Gentle exercise and elevation are encouraged.")
B.QW=s(["Myasthenia gravis","Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome","Dermatomyositis","Polymyositis"],t.s)
B.aaU=new A.e("Which paraneoplastic syndrome is associated with proximal muscle weakness that improves with repeated use?",B.QW,1,"Lambert-Eaton syndrome is a paraneoplastic condition (commonly associated with small cell lung cancer) featuring proximal weakness that characteristically improves with repeated use (opposite of myasthenia gravis). It involves antibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels.")
B.QX=s(["Humerus","Femur","Vertebra","Pelvis"],t.s)
B.a7n=new A.e("Pathologic fractures in cancer patients most commonly occur in which bone?",B.QX,1,"The femur is the most common site for pathologic fractures due to metastatic disease. The proximal femur bears significant weight-loading forces. Prophylactic fixation should be considered when >50% of cortical bone is destroyed.")
