B.RQ=s(["Rotator cuff insertion","Superior labrum from anterior to posterior","Inferior glenohumeral ligament","Coracoacromial ligament"],t.s)
B.a8q=new A.e("A SLAP lesion involves a tear of the:",B.RQ,1,"SLAP = Superior Labrum Anterior to Posterior. It involves the superior labrum at the biceps anchor. Common in overhead athletes and after falls on an outstretched hand. O'Brien's test is used for diagnosis.")
B.Xa=s(["Supraspinatus tendon","Biceps tendon (long head)","Subscapularis tendon","Acromioclavicular joint"],t.s)
B.a6p=new A.e("The Speed's test evaluates pathology of which structure?",B.Xa,1,"Speed's test: resist forward flexion with the elbow extended and forearm supinated. Pain at the anterior shoulder/bicipital groove = biceps tendinopathy or SLAP lesion.")
B.Rc=s(["Neer's test","Cross-body adduction test","Empty can test","Apprehension test"],t.s)
B.a7Z=new A.e("AC joint pathology is best assessed with which provocative test?",B.Rc,1,"The cross-body adduction (scarf) test: adduct the arm across the chest. Pain at the AC joint = positive for AC joint pathology (arthritis, separation, or distal clavicle osteolysis).")
B.WI=s(["Rotator cuff tear","AC joint arthritis","Anterior glenohumeral instability","Biceps tendinopathy"],t.s)
B.a4f=new A.e("The apprehension test is positive for:",B.WI,2,"The apprehension test: arm at 90\xb0 abduction and external rotation. The patient feels a sense of impending dislocation (apprehension, not just pain) = positive for anterior glenohumeral instability. The relocation test (posterior pressure on humeral head) should relieve the apprehension.")
B.Qy=s(["Deltoid and teres minor","Supraspinatus and infraspinatus","Subscapularis and teres major","Biceps and brachialis"],t.s)
B.a7x=new A.e("Suprascapular neuropathy causes weakness of which two muscles?",B.Qy,1,"The suprascapular nerve innervates the supraspinatus (abduction initiation) and infraspinatus (external rotation). Entrapment at the suprascapular notch affects both; entrapment at the spinoglenoid notch affects only infraspinatus.")
