B.a7s=new A.e("For tendon transfer surgery in SCI, what is the minimum muscle grade required for a donor muscle?",B.PW,1,"A donor muscle must be grade 4 or ideally grade 5 because the muscle loses one full grade of strength during the transfer process. A grade 3 muscle would become grade 2 (non-functional against gravity) after transfer.")
B.N1=s(["Deltoid to triceps","Brachioradialis to ECRB (extensor carpi radialis brevis)","Pronator teres to ECRL","FDS to EDC"],t.s)
B.aci=new A.e("What is the classic tendon transfer for a C5-level tetraplegic to restore wrist extension?",B.N1,1,"At C5 level, the brachioradialis (an elbow flexor made redundant by the intact biceps) is transferred to the ECRB to restore active wrist extension, enabling tenodesis grasp.")
B.UM=s(["The patient has normal two-point discrimination","The patient requires visual feedback to distinguish objects and cannot feel objects smaller than 10mm with eyes closed","The patient is completely insensate","The patient has intact light touch only"],t.s)
B.a9I=new A.e("In the upper limb classification for tendon transfers, what does the O (ocular) designation indicate about the patient's sensation?",B.UM,1,"O (ocular) means the patient requires visual feedback to distinguish objects. They cannot tell what they are holding if their eyes are closed for items smaller than 10mm. Q (cutaneous) indicates better sensation with two-point discrimination <10mm.")
B.X1=s(["Moberg key grip procedure","Brachioradialis to FPL transfer (BR to FPL)","Deltoid to triceps transfer","Zancolli lasso procedure"],t.s)
B.ab4=new A.e("What surgical procedure restores active lateral key grip at the C6 level by powering the thumb flexor?",B.X1,1,"The BR to FPL (brachioradialis to flexor pollicis longus) transfer provides an active lateral pinch at C6 level. Many surgeons prefer this over the Moberg procedure because it creates an active rather than static grip.")
B.ZK=s(["The arm is stuck in pronation; release the pronator teres","The biceps is unopposed as a supinator causing supination contracture; reroute the biceps tendon around the radial neck","The triceps overpowers the biceps; lengthen the triceps","Wrist drop from radial nerve palsy; tendon transfer"],t.s)
B.abm=new A.e("What supination problem occurs in C5-C6 injuries and what is the surgical correction?",B.ZK,1,"In C5-C6 injuries, the biceps is an unopposed powerful supinator (no working pronator to counteract it), causing the arm to get stuck in supination. The biceps tendon can be rerouted around the radial neck to correct this.")
B.UX=s([B.a7s,B.aci,B.a9I,B.ab4,B.abm],t.b)
B.Kc=new A.a8("Spinal Cord Injury 10",B.UX)
B.Uq=s(["Elderly women with osteoporosis","Adolescent athletes","Children ages 4-10 (peak 5-7)","Young adults on corticosteroids"],t.s)
B.a6n=new A.e("Legg-Calve-Perthes disease is avascular necrosis of the femoral head in which population?",B.Uq,2,"Legg-Calve-Perthes is idiopathic AVN of the femoral head in children ages 4-10, with peak at 5-7 years. It is 5x more common in boys. The child presents with a painless limp and limited hip abduction/internal rotation.")
B.PB=s(["Thin, athletic adolescent girls","Overweight adolescent boys (ages 10-16)","Toddlers learning to walk","Elderly patients after falls"],t.s)
B.a8J=new A.e("Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE) most commonly presents in which demographic?",B.PB,1,'SCFE occurs in overweight adolescents (10-16 years), more common in boys. The femoral head "slips" posteriorly and inferiorly relative to the femoral neck. Classic presentation: knee or groin pain with a limp. Obligate external rotation with flexion is pathognomonic.')
B.VC=s(["Greater trochanter","Lateral knee at the femoral epicondyle","Medial knee","Anterior hip"],t.s)
B.a9t=new A.e("Iliotibial band syndrome causes pain at which location?",B.VC,1,"IT band syndrome causes lateral knee pain at the femoral epicondyle, where the IT band snaps over the bony prominence during flexion-extension. It is common in runners and cyclists. Ober's test assesses IT band tightness.")
B.Z9=s(["Hamstring tightness","Hip flexion contracture","Sacroiliac joint dysfunction","Quadriceps weakness"],t.s)
B.acU=new A.e("The Thomas test assesses for:",B.Z9,1,"The Thomas test: supine, flex the contralateral hip to flatten the lumbar lordosis. If the tested leg rises off the table, a hip flexion contracture is present (tight iliopsoas). This is common in wheelchair users and patients with prolonged bed rest.")
B.SC=s(["The acetabular labrum","The greater trochanter (external type)","The iliopsoas tendon","The femoral head"],t.s)
B.aab=new A.e("Snapping hip syndrome (coxa saltans) from the iliotibial band involves snapping over which structure?",B.SC,1,"External snapping hip: the IT band or gluteus maximus snaps over the greater trochanter. Internal snapping hip: the iliopsoas tendon snaps over the iliopectineal eminence or femoral head. Intra-articular: labral tears or loose bodies.")
B.PC=s([B.a6n,B.a8J,B.a9t,B.acU,B.aab],t.b)
B.Kd=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Hip 3",B.PC)
B.RS=s(["Administer benzodiazepines","Apply physical restraints","Assess the environment and ensure safety through environmental modifications","Start maintenance medications such as SSRIs"],t.s)
B.a6y=new A.e("What is the first step in managing an agitated patient with TBI according to the hierarchy of intervention?",B.RS,2,"The source material emphasizes that the first step is not medication. You begin with environmental modifications such as floor beds, net beds, and safe rooms to allow agitation to occur without risk of injury.")
B.Xy=s(["It sedates the patient through dopamine depletion","It improves frontal lobe attention and processing speed, reducing confusion-driven agitation","It acts as a long-term anxiolytic","It directly blocks sympathetic overdrive"],t.s)
B.acA=new A.e("Why might a neurostimulant such as methylphenidate paradoxically reduce agitation in a TBI patient?",B.Xy,1,"Agitation often results from profound confusion due to poor attention and slow processing. A neurostimulant can help the frontal lobes function better, allowing the patient to understand their environment and reducing agitation.")
B.Qf=s(["50% to 75% of expected metabolic needs","75% to 100% of expected metabolic needs","100% to 140% of expected metabolic needs","140% to 200% of expected metabolic needs"],t.s)
B.a8Z=new A.e("What is the recommended caloric replacement level for a patient in the acute phase of TBI due to the hypermetabolic state?",B.Qf,2,"Acute TBI triggers a massive hypermetabolic and hypercatabolic state, with energy requirements jumping to 100% to 140% of expected levels due to catecholamine release, cortisol, and increased muscle tone.")
B.Zi=s(["Methylphenidate","Propranolol","Trazodone","Valproic acid"],t.s)
B.a5H=new A.e("Which medication is specifically recommended for sympathetic storming (tachycardia, hypertension, diaphoresis) in TBI patients?",B.Zi,1,"Propranolol, a beta blocker, is highlighted as a maintenance medication that is effective when there is a sympathetic overdrive component such as storming with tachycardia, hypertension, and diaphoresis.")
B.Ui=s(["Sundowning","Urinary tract infection","Iatrogenic withdrawal from sedatives such as fentanyl or midazolam","Undiagnosed seizure activity"],t.s)
B.a6N=new A.e("When evaluating an agitated TBI patient, which of the following is described as an often-missed cause of agitation related to medications given in the ICU?",B.Ui,2,"The transcript specifically highlights that iatrogenic withdrawal is frequently missed. Patients who received fentanyl and midazolam drips in the ICU for weeks and then had them abruptly discontinued may experience withdrawal from those same medications.")
