B.acC=new A.e("What is the primary target of the immune attack in multiple sclerosis?",B.Vm,1,"The primary target is the oligodendrocyte, the specialized glial cell that manufactures and maintains the myelin sheath in the CNS. One oligodendrocyte myelinates multiple axon segments.")
B.Qn=s(["MS always presents before age 15","If a person migrates from a high-risk to low-risk area before age 15, they acquire the lower risk of their new location","MS symptoms must be present for 15 years before diagnosis","Disability becomes permanent after 15 years"],t.s)
B.a99=new A.e("What is the 15-year rule in MS epidemiology?",B.Qn,1,"If a person migrates from a high-risk to a low-risk area before age 15, they acquire the lower MS risk. Migration after age 15 retains the original high risk, suggesting environmental exposure during childhood sets the immunological thermostat.")
B.WP=s(["HLA-B27","HLA-DRB1*1501 on chromosome 6","BRCA1","ApoE4"],t.s)
B.aaL=new A.e("What is the strongest genetic risk factor identified for MS?",B.WP,1,"The HLA-DRB1*1501 allele on chromosome 6 is the strongest genetic risk factor for MS. It is involved in immune system antigen presentation, consistent with an autoimmune disease.")
B.Ro=s(["3-5%","15%","39%","75%"],t.s)
B.a5A=new A.e("What is the concordance rate for MS in monozygotic (identical) twins?",B.Ro,2,"The concordance rate is 39% in identical twins. This proves both genetic susceptibility (much higher than the 3-5% for fraternal twins) and environmental influence (if purely genetic, it would be 100%).")
B.YZ=s(["Relapse rates increase throughout pregnancy","Relapse rates decrease during pregnancy (especially third trimester) but spike in the first 3-6 months postpartum","Pregnancy has no effect on relapse rates","Relapse rates increase only in the first trimester"],t.s)
B.a9h=new A.e("How does pregnancy affect MS relapse rates?",B.YZ,1,"During pregnancy, the immune system shifts from pro-inflammatory TH1 to anti-inflammatory TH2, decreasing relapses. In the first 3-6 months postpartum, relapses spike as the immune system rebounds. Long-term prognosis is unchanged.")
