B.Oo=s(["Both elevated","PTH elevated, vitamin D low","Both suppressed (low)","PTH low, vitamin D elevated"],t.s)
B.a9L=new A.e("In immobilization hypercalcemia following spinal cord injury, what are the expected parathyroid hormone (PTH) and vitamin D levels?",B.Oo,2,"In immobilization hypercalcemia, the body recognizes high calcium and tries to suppress it, so both PTH and vitamin D levels are low. This distinguishes it from hyperparathyroidism.")
B.Z8=s(["Calcitonin; peaks at 24 hours","Pamidronate; peaks at approximately 7 days","Hydrochlorothiazide; peaks at 3 days","IV calcium gluconate; peaks at 1 hour"],t.s)
B.a8C=new A.e("Which medication is the definitive treatment for immobilization hypercalcemia in SCI, and approximately how long does it take to reach peak effect?",B.Z8,1,"Pamidronate, a bisphosphonate, inhibits osteoclasts and is the definitive treatment. It takes about 3 days to see a drop in calcium, with peak effect at approximately 7 days. Thiazides are contraindicated as they increase calcium reabsorption.")
B.Tp=s(["The intercostal muscles function better when supine","Gravity pushes the flaccid abdominal contents up against the diaphragm, optimizing its length-tension relationship","Supine position increases airway diameter","Lung compliance is higher in the supine position"],t.s)
B.aco=new A.e("Why do tetraplegic patients breathe better in the supine position compared to sitting upright?",B.Tp,1,"In tetraplegia, flaccid abdominal muscles provide no support when sitting. Lying supine allows gravity to push abdominal contents up against the diaphragm, pre-stretching it to an optimal length-tension relationship for more efficient contraction.")
