B.N5=s(["120/80 mmHg","130/80 mmHg","140/90 mmHg","150/90 mmHg"],t.s)
B.a5b=new A.e("According to current guidelines, at what blood pressure threshold should treatment be initiated for cardiac rehab patients?",B.N5,1,"The 2017 guidelines shifted the treatment threshold from 140/90 to the more aggressive target of 130/80 mmHg. This is a commonly tested board question as the guidelines have changed.")
B.Tw=s(["5-10%","11-20%","30-40%","50-60%"],t.s)
B.a6w=new A.e("What percentage of eligible candidates actually participate in cardiac rehabilitation programs?",B.Tw,1,"Despite undeniable survival benefits, only 11-20% of eligible candidates participate in cardiac rehab. This participation gap disproportionately affects older people, females, and non-white populations.")
B.Ug=s(["Male sex","Age (men >45, women >55)","Diabetes mellitus","Family history of premature CAD"],t.s)
B.a7O=new A.e("Which of the following is NOT a non-modifiable risk factor for coronary artery disease?",B.Ug,2,"Diabetes is a modifiable risk factor. Non-modifiable risk factors include: age (men >45, women >55), male sex, family history (male first-degree relative with CAD <55, female <65), and personal history of vascular disease.")
B.U2=s(["Surgical recovery","Vocational status","Pharmaceutical management","Diagnostic testing"],t.s)
B.aat=new A.e("In the formal definition of cardiac rehabilitation, which component is often overlooked but specifically included as a goal?",B.U2,1,"The formal definition includes restoring optimal physiological, psychological, social, vocational, and emotional status. The vocational component is a key board concept \u2014 rehab aims to return patients to a productive role in society, not just survival.")
B.Yl=s([B.a5U,B.a5b,B.a6w,B.a7O,B.aat],t.b)
B.Ko=new A.a8("Cardiac Rehab 1",B.Yl)
B.R1=s(["Spasticity is painful while rigidity is not","Spasticity is velocity dependent while rigidity is velocity independent","Spasticity affects the upper extremities while rigidity affects the lower extremities","Spasticity is associated with lower motor neuron lesions while rigidity is associated with upper motor neuron lesions"],t.s)
B.a7L=new A.e("What is the key feature that distinguishes spasticity from rigidity on physical examination?",B.R1,1,"Spasticity is defined by a velocity-dependent increase in tonic stretch reflexes -- the faster you stretch the muscle, the greater the resistance. Rigidity shows constant resistance regardless of speed (velocity independent), as seen in Parkinson's disease.")
B.Zd=s(["A fixed bony contracture requiring surgery","Dynamic neural spasticity with potential for improvement from Botox or medications","Normal muscle tone with no treatment needed","Severe rigidity from basal ganglia disease"],t.s)
B.acS=new A.e("In the modified Tardieu scale, what does a large difference between R1 and R2 angles indicate?",B.Zd,1,"A large R1-R2 difference means the joint catches early with fast stretch (R1) but moves much further with slow stretch (R2). This gap represents the dynamic, neural component of spasticity that can be treated with Botox or medications. A small gap suggests fixed contracture.")
B.PU=s(["Alpha motor neurons","Gamma motor neurons","Ia inhibitory interneurons","Dorsal root ganglion cells"],t.s)
B.abp=new A.e("According to the current prevailing theory, spasticity results from a loss of descending facilitatory influences on which spinal cord structure?",B.PU,2,"The current theory is that spasticity results from loss of descending facilitatory input to the Ia inhibitory interneuron. The brain normally facilitates (activates) this inhibitory interneuron. When that signal is lost, the inhibitor stops inhibiting, and spinal reflexes become hyperactive.")
B.Z5=s(["Spasticity always resolves spontaneously within 6 months","The spasticity may be functionally useful, such as providing extensor tone that allows standing or transfers","Anti-spasticity medications are not available for outpatient use","Spasticity treatment is only indicated for pediatric patients"],t.s)
B.a4J=new A.e("Why might a clinician choose NOT to treat a patient's spasticity even though it is clearly present?",B.Z5,1,"Extensor spasticity can act as a natural splint, allowing patients with weak legs to stand and transfer. Eliminating this tone may reveal profound underlying weakness and worsen function. Treatment is only indicated when spasticity interferes with function or causes complications.")
B.U8=s(["Grade 2 has clonus while grade 3 does not","Grade 2 shows resistance through most of the range but the limb is easily moved; grade 3 shows considerable tone and passive movement is difficult","Grade 2 occurs only at end range while grade 3 occurs throughout","Grade 2 is velocity dependent while grade 3 is velocity independent"],t.s)
B.a4d=new A.e("On the Modified Ashworth Scale, what distinguishes a grade 2 from a grade 3?",B.U8,1,"The key distinction is that at grade 2, there is a marked increase in tone through most of the range but the affected part is still easily moved. At grade 3, there is a considerable increase in tone and passive movement is difficult.")
B.U4=s([B.a7L,B.acS,B.abp,B.a4J,B.a4d],t.b)
B.Kp=new A.a8("Spasticity 1",B.U4)
B.Tr=s(["T4","T6","T8","T10"],t.s)
B.a94=new A.e("What spinal level is the critical watershed for autonomic dysreflexia and orthostatic hypotension?",B.Tr,1,"T6 is the critical watershed because the splanchnic outflow (T5-L2) controls the massive abdominal vascular bed. Injuries at T6 and above sever brain control over this major blood pressure regulator.")
B.a_p=s(["T-eight = temper-eight","Temp-8 (temperature)","Eight = heat","Therm-8"],t.s)
B.a8g=new A.e("What is the mnemonic for remembering T8 as the cutoff for temperature regulation?",B.a_p,1,"The mnemonic is Temp-8, spelling temperature with the number 8 to remember that T8 is the critical level for thermoregulation. Patients with injuries above T8 are poikilothermic.")
B.SI=s(["Administer nitropaste","Check the bladder","Sit the patient up immediately","Give nifedipine"],t.s)
B.a5p=new A.e("In autonomic dysreflexia, what is the very first step in management?",B.SI,2,"The first step is to sit the patient up immediately to use gravity to lower blood pressure, and loosen any tight clothing. Then identify and remove the noxious stimulus, starting with the bladder.")
B.Nq=s(["Fludrocortisone; avoid with renal failure","Midodrine; do not give the last dose too close to bedtime","Droxidopa; avoid with liver disease","Ephedrine; monitor for tachycardia"],t.s)
B.a9e=new A.e("Which alpha-1 agonist is a staple medication for treating orthostatic hypotension in SCI, and what is the important dosing precaution?",B.Nq,1,"Midodrine (ProAmatine) is an alpha-1 agonist used for orthostatic hypotension. The last dose should not be given too close to bedtime because it can cause supine hypertension when the patient lies flat.")
B.Wr=s(["Systolic rise of 10 mmHg above baseline","Systolic rise of 20 to 40 mmHg above baseline","Systolic BP greater than 140/90","Systolic rise of 60 mmHg above baseline"],t.s)
B.a8M=new A.e("What is the definition of autonomic dysreflexia in terms of blood pressure change from baseline?",B.Wr,1,"AD is defined as a systolic rise of just 20-40 mmHg above baseline. Since SCI patients often have low baseline BP (e.g., 90 systolic), a reading of 130 could represent a hypertensive crisis for them.")
B.VE=s([B.a94,B.a8g,B.a5p,B.a9e,B.a8M],t.b)
B.Kq=new A.a8("Spinal Cord Injury 5",B.VE)
B.Zf=s(["Intercostals work better supine","Gravity pushes abdominal contents up against the diaphragm, restoring optimal length-tension","Accessory muscles are more active supine","Lung compliance increases supine"],t.s)
B.ab6=new A.e("In a C5 tetraplegic, why is vital capacity higher supine than sitting?",B.Zf,1,"Supine positioning pushes abdominal contents up against the diaphragm, restoring its domed position and optimal length-tension relationship.")
B.Pu=s(["50 to 100 mL/year","200 to 250 mL/year","500 to 750 mL/year","1000 mL/year"],t.s)
B.a8H=new A.e("At what rate does vital capacity decline annually in Duchenne muscular dystrophy after the plateau?",B.Pu,1,"Vital capacity is lost at roughly 200-250 mL per year after the plateau phase (ages 10-15).")
B.Y6=s(["Sodium","Phosphate","Chloride","Bicarbonate"],t.s)
B.a3N=new A.e("Which electrolyte deficiency specifically causes respiratory muscle weakness in COPD?",B.Y6,1,"Hypophosphatemia is key because phosphate is a component of ATP, the energy currency for muscle contraction.")
B.U5=s(["0.8 g/kg/day","1.2 g/kg/day","1.7 g/kg/day or greater","2.5 g/kg/day"],t.s)
B.a4t=new A.e("What daily protein requirement is recommended for COPD patients?",B.U5,2,"Protein needs to be \u22651.7 g/kg/day because the metabolic cost of breathing in severe COPD can consume 25-30% of total energy.")
B.PL=s(["Bronchodilation, anti-inflammatory, mucolytic","Bronchodilation, increased diaphragmatic contractility, improved mucociliary clearance","Anti-tussive, bronchodilation, sedation","Vasodilation, anti-inflammatory, bronchodilation"],t.s)
B.aa_=new A.e("What three simultaneous effects make theophylline unique among COPD medications?",B.PL,1,"Theophylline simultaneously bronchodilates, increases diaphragmatic contractility/fatigue resistance, and speeds mucociliary clearance.")
B.VJ=s([B.ab6,B.a8H,B.a3N,B.a4t,B.aa_],t.b)
B.Kr=new A.a8("Pulmonary Rehab 2",B.VJ)
B.QB=s(["Motor vehicle accidents","Falls","Firearm injuries","Sports-related injuries"],t.s)
B.a4n=new A.e("What is the leading cause of TBI-related emergency department visits and hospitalizations?",B.QB,1,"Falls account for 48% of TBI-related ED visits and 52% of hospitalizations, making them the number one cause overall.")
B.PX=s(["Ages 0 to 5","Ages 15 to 24","Ages 25 to 44","Ages 65 and older"],t.s)
B.a66=new A.e("TBI has a bimodal age distribution. Which age group has the highest mortality rate?",B.PX,3,"The elderly population aged 65 and older has the highest mortality rate due to reduced physiological reserve, comorbidities, anticoagulant use, and brain atrophy.")
B.St=s(["1 to 1","1.5 to 1","2.5 to 1","5 to 1"],t.s)
B.ad1=new A.e("What is the approximate male-to-female ratio for TBI incidence?",B.St,2,"The male-to-female ratio for TBI is roughly 2.5 to 1, with males accounting for about 74% of TBIs according to model system data.")
B.Zg=s(["1.2 million","2.8 million","5.3 million","8.1 million"],t.s)
B.a7e=new A.e("According to CDC data, approximately how many Americans are currently living with TBI-related disabilities?",B.Zg,2,"Approximately 5.3 million Americans are currently living with TBI-related disabilities, representing a large chronic disease population.")
B.MC=s(["Head-on collision","T-bone impact at an intersection","Ejection from the vehicle","Rollover without ejection"],t.s)
B.a98=new A.e("What is the single most common cause of death or severe injury in a motor vehicle accident?",B.MC,2,"Ejection from the vehicle is the single most common cause of death or severe injury in an MVA because the forces involved are exponential.")
B.NW=s([B.a4n,B.a66,B.ad1,B.a7e,B.a98],t.b)
B.Ks=new A.a8("Traumatic Brain Injury 1",B.NW)
B.Tf=s(["Continuous propagation of action potentials along unmyelinated fibers","Action potentials jumping from one node of Ranvier to the next","Conduction across the neuromuscular junction","Retrograde conduction in sensory nerves"],t.s)
B.a6O=new A.e("Saltatory conduction refers to:",B.Tf,1,'Saltatory conduction is the process by which action potentials "jump" between nodes of Ranvier in myelinated nerves. This dramatically increases conduction velocity (up to 120 m/s) and reduces energy expenditure compared to continuous conduction in unmyelinated fibers.')
B.Vh=s(["-40 mV","-55 mV","-70 mV","-90 mV"],t.s)
B.a6B=new A.e("The resting membrane potential of a typical neuron is approximately:",B.Vh,2,"The resting membrane potential is approximately -70 mV, maintained primarily by the Na+/K+ ATPase pump (3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in) and potassium leak channels. This negative intracellular charge is essential for action potential generation.")
B.Xm=s(["Potassium channels","Calcium channels","Chloride channels","Sodium channels"],t.s)
B.a8k=new A.e("During depolarization of an action potential, which ion channels open first?",B.Xm,3,"Voltage-gated sodium channels open first during depolarization, allowing rapid Na+ influx that drives the membrane potential from -70 mV toward +30 mV. Potassium channels open later for repolarization. This sequence is the basis of the action potential.")
B.Uv=s(["Norepinephrine","GABA","Glutamate","Acetylcholine"],t.s)
B.add=new A.e("The neuromuscular junction uses which neurotransmitter?",B.Uv,3,"Acetylcholine (ACh) is the neurotransmitter at the neuromuscular junction. It is released from the presynaptic terminal, crosses the synaptic cleft, and binds to nicotinic receptors on the motor end plate, triggering muscle contraction.")
B.Zj=s(["Increased conduction velocity and decreased amplitude","Decreased conduction velocity and increased amplitude","No change in conduction parameters","Decreased conduction velocity and decreased amplitude"],t.s)
B.acL=new A.e("Cooling a nerve will cause which of the following changes on nerve conduction studies?",B.Zj,1,"Cool temperatures slow sodium channel kinetics, prolonging the action potential duration. This decreases conduction velocity but paradoxically increases amplitude (the prolonged action potential allows more temporal summation). This can mimic demyelinating neuropathy \u2014 always check limb temperature!")
B.Ue=s([B.a6O,B.a6B,B.a8k,B.add,B.acL],t.b)
B.Kt=new A.a8("Electrodiagnostic 2",B.Ue)
B.Wu=s(["Sensory loss in the thumb and index finger",'Pure motor deficit with inability to form an "OK" sign',"Wrist drop with radial deviation","Thenar atrophy and thumb opposition weakness"],t.s)
B.a5l=new A.e("Anterior interosseous nerve (AIN) syndrome is characterized by:",B.Wu,1,'AIN syndrome is pure motor \u2014 NO sensory findings. It causes weakness of FPL (thumb IP flexion), FDP to digits 2-3 (index/middle DIP flexion), and pronator quadratus. The positive "OK sign" test (can\'t make a circle) is characteristic. Routine median NCS are NORMAL.')
B.Qq=s(["Absent","Decreased","Normal (preserved)","Hyperesthetic"],t.s)
B.aa9=new A.e("In carpal tunnel syndrome, sensation at the base of the thumb is typically:",B.Qq,2,"The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve exits 5 cm proximal to the wrist and travels OVER the transverse carpal ligament, not through the tunnel. Therefore, thenar eminence/thumb base sensation is preserved in CTS. If numb, suspect a more proximal lesion.")
B.WL=s(["5 tendons + median nerve","7 tendons + median nerve","9 tendons + median nerve","11 tendons + median nerve"],t.s)
B.aaK=new A.e("The carpal tunnel contains how many tendons plus the median nerve?",B.WL,2,'The "Nine Plus One Rule": 4 FDS tendons + 4 FDP tendons + 1 FPL tendon + 1 median nerve = 10 structures. The FCR tendon runs in its own separate compartment, which is why wrist flexion is preserved in carpal tunnel syndrome.')
B.a_0=s(["Radial to ulnar","Median to ulnar","Ulnar to median","Musculocutaneous to median"],t.s)
B.acb=new A.e("Martin-Gruber anastomosis is a crossover between which two nerves in the forearm?",B.a_0,1,"Martin-Gruber anastomosis (15-20% prevalence) is a median-to-ulnar nerve crossover in the forearm. Motor fibers from the median nerve cross to join the ulnar nerve. This can cause confusing NCS results in carpal tunnel or ulnar neuropathy.")
B.WZ=s(["Prolonged sensory latency with normal amplitude","Normal sensory and motor studies","Absent SNAP, prolonged/low motor response, fibrillations in APB","Prolonged sensory latency only"],t.s)
B.acj=new A.e("Severe carpal tunnel syndrome on NCS is characterized by:",B.WZ,2,"CTS severity grading: Mild = prolonged sensory latency only. Moderate = abnormal sensory + prolonged motor latency. Severe = absent SNAP, low/prolonged motor response, and fibrillation potentials in the abductor pollicis brevis (APB) on EMG.")
B.Wh=s([B.a5l,B.aa9,B.aaK,B.acb,B.acj],t.b)
B.Ku=new A.a8("Electrodiagnostic 8",B.Wh)
B.OJ=s(["Constant high fever","Quotidian (daily) intermittent spikes that return completely to baseline, often with an evanescent salmon-colored rash","Low-grade fever that persists for weeks","Fever only at night"],t.s)
B.aaC=new A.e("What is the hallmark fever pattern of systemic JIA (Still's disease)?",B.OJ,1,"Systemic JIA features a quotidian or double quotidian intermittent fever that spikes once or twice daily and returns completely to baseline. It is paired with an evanescent, non-pruritic, salmon-colored rash that appears and disappears with the fever.")
B.PN=s(["RF positive","ANA positive","HLA-B27 positive","High ESR"],t.s)
B.a5P=new A.e("In oligoarticular JIA, what combination puts a child at highest risk for silent chronic uveitis requiring slit-lamp screening every 3 months?",B.PN,1,"ANA-positive oligoarticular JIA patients have significantly greater risk of chronic, asymptomatic anterior uveitis that can cause blindness. They need slit-lamp exams every 3 months for at least 4-5 years.")
B.Sr=s(["Systemic JIA","Polyarticular JIA, RF positive","Oligoarticular JIA","Polyarticular JIA, RF negative"],t.s)
B.aaA=new A.e("Which JIA subtype has the worst prognosis and resembles adult RA?",B.Sr,1,"RF-positive polyarticular JIA (5-10% of cases, typically teenage girls) behaves exactly like adult RA - erosive, unremitting, with subcutaneous nodules. It has the worst prognosis of all JIA subtypes.")
B.a_n=s(["Two weeks","Four weeks","Six weeks","Three months"],t.s)
B.ac9=new A.e("In the diagnosis of JIA, arthritis must be present in one or more joints for at least how long?",B.a_n,2,"The ACR criteria require arthritis in one or more joints for at least six weeks in a child under 16. This duration distinguishes chronic inflammatory arthritis from transient viral or traumatic causes.")
B.NQ=s(["Seropositive Erosive Arthropathy; RF positive","Seronegative Enthesopathy and Arthropathy; HLA-B27 positive","Systemic Eosinophilic Arthritis; ANA positive","Subacute Erosive Arthritis; HLA-DR4 positive"],t.s)
B.a8D=new A.e("What does the SEA syndrome acronym stand for, and what genetic marker is typically positive?",B.NQ,1,"SEA stands for Seronegative Enthesopathy and Arthropathy. These children are RF and ANA negative but HLA-B27 positive, and many eventually develop full-blown ankylosing spondylitis.")
B.PF=s([B.aaC,B.a5P,B.aaA,B.ac9,B.a8D],t.b)
B.Kv=new A.a8("Rheumatology 3",B.PF)
B.Ra=s(["Isolated ACL tear","Severe tricompartmental osteoarthritis failing conservative management","Osgood-Schlatter disease","Acute meniscal tear"],t.s)
B.a73=new A.e("Total knee arthroplasty is indicated for which of the following?",B.Ra,1,"TKA is indicated for severe symptomatic OA (typically tricompartmental) that has failed conservative treatment including weight loss, PT, injections, and medications. Unicompartmental (partial) knee replacement may be considered for isolated medial or lateral compartment disease.")
B.Yk=s(["60\xb0","90\xb0","120\xb0","135\xb0"],t.s)
B.a7D=new A.e("After total knee arthroplasty, achieving which degree of knee flexion is considered a functional goal?",B.Yk,2,"A functional goal of 120\xb0 flexion allows patients to perform most daily activities. Minimum needed: 67\xb0 for swing phase of gait, 83\xb0 for stairs, 93\xb0 to rise from a chair, ~105-110\xb0 for bathing. 120\xb0 is an excellent outcome.")
B.MU=s(["ACL tear","Repeated kneeling on a hard surface","Quadriceps tendon rupture","Meniscal degeneration"],t.s)
B.a5y=new A.e('Prepatellar bursitis ("housemaid\'s knee") is caused by:',B.MU,1,"Prepatellar bursitis results from repetitive friction/pressure over the anterior patella, classically from kneeling (housemaid, roofer, plumber). Septic bursitis must be excluded by aspiration if warmth, erythema, or fever are present.")
B.SQ=s(["ACL","PCL","MCL (medial collateral ligament)","LCL (lateral collateral ligament)"],t.s)
B.acI=new A.e("The valgus stress test at the knee evaluates which structure?",B.SQ,2,"Valgus stress test: apply a lateral-to-medial force at the knee (pushing the knee medially). Laxity at 30\xb0 flexion = isolated MCL injury. Laxity at full extension = MCL + possible cruciate/capsular involvement (more severe).")
B.QE=s(["ACL tear","MCL sprain","Bucket-handle meniscal tear","Patellofemoral syndrome"],t.s)
B.a8s=new A.e("A locked knee that cannot fully extend is most likely caused by:",B.QE,2,'A bucket-handle meniscal tear is a displaced vertical longitudinal tear that flips into the intercondylar notch, mechanically blocking full extension ("locked knee"). This is a surgical urgency requiring arthroscopic reduction and repair/resection.')
B.Tj=s([B.a73,B.a7D,B.a5y,B.acI,B.a8s],t.b)
B.Kw=new A.a8("Musculoskeletal: Knee 3",B.Tj)
B.UN=s(["RA, hepatomegaly, thrombocytopenia","RA, splenomegaly, neutropenia (leukopenia)","RA, lymphadenopathy, anemia","RA, pericarditis, pleuritis"],t.s)
B.aaN=new A.e("What is the triad of Felty syndrome?",B.UN,1,"Felty syndrome is the classic triad of rheumatoid arthritis, splenomegaly (big spleen), and leukopenia (specifically neutropenia). It typically occurs in patients with longstanding RA (>10 years).")
B.Qh=s(["Chronic neck pain only","Quadriparesis or death from spinal cord compression","Vertebral artery dissection","Cranial nerve palsies"],t.s)
B.a5Q=new A.e("What is the critical danger of atlantoaxial (C1-C2) instability in RA patients?",B.Qh,1,"RA inflammation erodes the transverse ligament and the dens, allowing posterior displacement that can compress the cervical spinal cord. Consequences include quadriparesis or death, making it the most dangerous complication to screen for.")
B.Uc=s(["Rheumatoid nodules in the throat","Cricoarytenoid joint involvement causing hoarseness and potential airway obstruction","Tracheomalacia","Laryngeal amyloidosis"],t.s)
B.a4g=new A.e("What RA complication involves the cricoarytenoid joint and can cause airway compromise?",B.Uc,1,"The cricoarytenoid joint is a synovial joint that RA can attack, causing hoarseness, dysphagia, and potentially life-threatening airway obstruction if the vocal cords become immobile.")
B.V6=s(["RA with interstitial lung disease","RA combined with pneumoconiosis (coal dust exposure) causing large intrapulmonary nodules","RA with pulmonary hypertension","RA with bronchiectasis"],t.s)
B.a50=new A.e("What is Caplan syndrome?",B.V6,1,"Caplan syndrome is the intersection of RA and pneumoconiosis (specifically in coal workers). The hyper-revved immune system mounts an exaggerated granulomatous response to inhaled silica/coal dust, creating massive intrapulmonary nodules.")
B.X2=s(["Complete immobilization for two weeks","Aggressive active range of motion","Passive range of motion at least twice daily to prevent contractures","Isometric strengthening exercises only"],t.s)
B.a7f=new A.e("During an acute RA flare, what is the critical exercise recommendation?",B.X2,2,"During acute flares, relative rest is recommended but passive range of motion must be performed at least twice daily to prevent contractures from forming. Complete immobilization leads to stiffness and loss of function.")
B.XI=s([B.aaN,B.a5Q,B.a4g,B.a50,B.a7f],t.b)
B.Kx=new A.a8("Rheumatology 2",B.XI)
B.N0=s(["Anterior spinal artery","Posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA)","Anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA)","Superior cerebellar artery"],t.s)
B.a7I=new A.e("Wallenberg syndrome (lateral medullary syndrome) results from occlusion of which artery?",B.N0,1,"Wallenberg syndrome is caused by PICA occlusion affecting the lateral medulla. Classic findings include ipsilateral facial pain/temperature loss, contralateral body pain/temperature loss, dysphagia, hoarseness, vertigo, and ipsilateral Horner syndrome.")
B.RT=s(["Weber syndrome","Wallenberg syndrome","Locked-in syndrome","Benedikt syndrome"],t.s)
B.a4L=new A.e("A patient presents with ipsilateral facial numbness, contralateral body numbness, dysphagia, hoarseness, and Horner syndrome. What is the most likely diagnosis?",B.RT,1,"This is the classic presentation of Wallenberg (lateral medullary) syndrome. The crossed sensory findings (ipsilateral face, contralateral body) are pathognomonic and a favorite board question.")
B.OX=s(["Lateral medulla","Ventral pons","Midbrain","Thalamus"],t.s)
B.acQ=new A.e("Locked-in syndrome is caused by a lesion in which structure?",B.OX,1,"Locked-in syndrome results from a ventral pontine lesion (typically basilar artery occlusion). The patient is fully conscious but quadriplegic and mute, retaining only vertical eye movements and blinking. Cognition is completely intact.")
B.SB=s(["Pons","Medulla","Midbrain","Cerebellum"],t.s)
B.a8p=new A.e("Weber syndrome is characterized by ipsilateral CN III palsy and contralateral hemiparesis. The lesion is located in which structure?",B.SB,2,'Weber syndrome is a midbrain syndrome caused by damage to the cerebral peduncle and CN III fascicle. It produces ipsilateral oculomotor palsy (ptosis, dilated pupil, "down and out" eye) and contralateral hemiparesis.')
B.Z0=s(["Thalamus","Posterior limb of the internal capsule","Caudate nucleus","Cerebral cortex"],t.s)
B.a7Y=new A.e("A lacunar infarct causing pure motor hemiparesis is most commonly located in which structure?",B.Z0,1,"Pure motor hemiparesis is the most common lacunar syndrome. The lesion is typically in the posterior limb of the internal capsule or basis pontis. It produces contralateral face, arm, and leg weakness without sensory, visual, or cognitive deficits.")
B.a_f=s([B.a7I,B.a4L,B.acQ,B.a8p,B.a7Y],t.b)
B.Ky=new A.a8("Cerebrovascular Accident 3",B.a_f)
B.X_=s(["Day 1","Day 3","Day 7","Day 14"],t.s)
B.a6e=new A.e("After a facial nerve injury, prognostic NCS testing should be delayed until at least:",B.X_,2,'The "seven-day rule": the distal nerve stump remains excitable for 3-7 days after injury due to Wallerian degeneration timeline. Testing before day 7 may show falsely normal responses, giving inaccurate prognosis.')
B.Tg=s(["Excellent prognosis, recovery in weeks","Good prognosis, recovery in 2 months","Fair prognosis, recovery in 2-8 months","Poor prognosis, recovery may take over 1 year"],t.s)
B.a3U=new A.e("A facial nerve CMAP amplitude less than 10% of the normal side indicates:",B.Tg,3,"Facial nerve prognosis by CMAP amplitude: >30% = good (recovery <2 months), 10-30% = fair (2-8 months), <10% = poor (>1 year recovery). This guides counseling and surgical decision-making.")
B.UQ=s(["Anterior triangle of the neck at the thyroid cartilage","Posterior border of the SCM, 3 cm above the clavicle","Suprasternal notch","Axilla at the level of the second rib"],t.s)
B.a7c=new A.e("The phrenic nerve is stimulated at which anatomical landmark for NCS?",B.UQ,1,"The phrenic nerve is stimulated at the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid (SCM) muscle, approximately 3 cm above the clavicle. Recording is at the xiphoid process with a 16 cm electrode distance.")
B.SA=s(["Brachial plexus","Cervical spinal cord","Thalamus","Primary somatosensory cortex"],t.s)
B.a9H=new A.e("In somatosensory evoked potentials (SSEPs) for the median nerve, the N20 waveform represents arrival at which structure?",B.SA,3,"Median nerve SSEP checkpoints: N9 = brachial plexus (Erb's point), N11 = root entry zone, N13 = cervical cord/medulla, P14 = brainstem, N18 = thalamus, N20 = primary somatosensory cortex. The number roughly corresponds to milliseconds.")
B.a_s=s(["Propofol and narcotics","Halogenated anesthetics (sevoflurane, isoflurane)","Ketamine","Dexmedetomidine"],t.s)
B.a8A=new A.e("During intraoperative SSEP monitoring, which anesthetic agents should be avoided because they suppress cortical signals?",B.a_s,1,"Halogenated anesthetics (sevoflurane, isoflurane, desflurane) suppress cortical SSEP signals and can mimic CNS injury. Total intravenous anesthesia (TIVA) with propofol and narcotics is preferred for intraoperative neuromonitoring.")
